window.PHYS={"unit":{"unit":3,"app":"Anatomy Practice","key":"anatomy.practice.bio210.u3.v1","title":"The Nervous System and the Senses","chapters":"Sections 14 to 19 and Practical 3","course":"BIO 210 Human Anatomy","teacher":"Dr. Khan Quintana","kc_word":"Key Concept","order_blurb":"The CSF path, the reflex arc, light and sound, the twelve cranial nerves","exam":{"name":"Exam 3 + Practical 3","date":"2026-11-02","label":"Mon Nov 2"},"units":[{"label":"← Unit 2","title":"Go back to Unit 2: bones, joints and muscles","href":"unit2.html"}]},"groups":[["s14","Section 14 · The spinal cord"],["s15","Section 15 · The brain"],["s16","Section 16 · Cranial nerves and the sheep brain"],["s17","Section 17 · Spinal nerves, plexuses and reflexes"],["s18","Section 18 · Autonomic division and the general senses"],["s19","Section 19 · Vision, hearing and balance"],["s13","Lab 13 · Nervous tissue (on Practical 3)"]],"topics":[{"id":"kcn1","ch":"s14","title":"Where the cord ends","core":"The adult spinal cord ends around L1 to L2 in a taper called the conus medullaris. Below it, the lumbar and sacral nerve roots continue down the canal as the cauda equina (\"horse's tail\"), anchored by the filum terminale. A lumbar puncture goes in below L2, into fluid around floating roots, so the needle cannot hit the cord.","trap":"Thinking the cord runs the whole length of the spine. It stops at L1 to L2; the canal below holds roots, not cord.","q":"Where does the adult spinal cord end, and what continues below it?","a":"Around L1 to L2, at the conus medullaris. The cauda equina, a bundle of nerve roots, continues below."},{"id":"kcn2","ch":"s14","title":"The cord in cross section","core":"Gray matter is the inner butterfly: dorsal (posterior) horns receive sensory signals, ventral (anterior) horns send motor commands, and lateral horns (T1 to L2) hold autonomic cell bodies. The two sides meet at the gray commissure around the central canal. White matter wraps the outside in dorsal, lateral and ventral columns (funiculi) of myelinated tracts.","trap":"Putting gray matter outside, as in the brain. In the cord it is inside; the white matter cables run around it.","q":"In a cord cross section, what does the dorsal horn handle, and what does the ventral horn handle?","a":"Dorsal horn: incoming sensory signals. Ventral horn: outgoing motor commands."},{"id":"kcn3","ch":"s14","title":"Meninges and the spaces between them","core":"Three meninges in the same order around brain and cord: dura mater (tough, outermost), arachnoid mater (web-like, middle), pia mater (hugs the tissue). The epidural space lies outside the dura and, along the spine, holds fat and vessels. Cerebrospinal fluid circulates in the subarachnoid space, between the arachnoid and the pia.","trap":"Putting CSF in the epidural space because \"epidural\" sounds central. CSF is in the subarachnoid space.","q":"Name the meninges from outside in. Where does CSF circulate?","a":"Dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater. CSF is in the subarachnoid space, between arachnoid and pia."},{"id":"kcn4","ch":"s14","title":"Roots, ganglion, spinal nerve, rami","core":"The dorsal root carries sensory information in; its cell bodies sit in the dorsal root ganglion just outside the cord. The ventral root carries motor commands out. They merge into a short spinal nerve, a mixed nerve. Past the vertebra it splits into a dorsal ramus (skin and muscles of the back) and a ventral ramus (which forms the plexuses).","trap":"Calling the spinal nerve purely sensory or purely motor. The roots are one-way; the spinal nerve is mixed.","q":"Which root carries sensory information, and where are those neurons' cell bodies?","a":"The dorsal root. Their cell bodies are in the dorsal root ganglion."},{"id":"kcn5","ch":"s14","title":"The wrappings of a nerve","core":"From inside out: endoneurium wraps each axon, perineurium wraps each fascicle (a bundle of axons), and epineurium wraps the whole nerve.","trap":"Mixing up perineurium and epineurium. \"Peri\" goes around a fascicle; \"epi\" is on the outside of the whole nerve.","q":"From a single axon out to the whole nerve, which connective tissue layer wraps what?","a":"Endoneurium (each axon), perineurium (each fascicle), epineurium (the whole nerve)."},{"id":"kcn6","ch":"s15","title":"Four regions, four lobes","core":"The brain's four major regions: cerebrum (thinking, voluntary movement, perception), diencephalon (thalamus, hypothalamus), brainstem (midbrain, pons, medulla: vital functions) and cerebellum (coordination). The cerebrum's lobes: frontal, parietal, temporal, occipital, plus the insula hidden in the lateral sulcus. The central sulcus divides frontal from parietal; the lateral sulcus sets off the temporal lobe; the longitudinal fissure splits the hemispheres.","trap":"Expecting to see the insula on the surface. It is buried beneath the frontal, parietal and temporal lobes.","q":"Name the four major brain regions and one job for each.","a":"Cerebrum (thinking, voluntary movement), diencephalon (sensory relay, homeostasis), brainstem (breathing, heart rate), cerebellum (coordination)."},{"id":"kcn7","ch":"s15","title":"Motor in front, sensory behind","core":"The precentral gyrus, just in front of the central sulcus, is the primary motor cortex. The postcentral gyrus, just behind it, is the primary somatosensory cortex. Precentral moves you; postcentral feels for you.","trap":"Swapping them. \"Pre\" comes before the central sulcus, in the frontal lobe, and moves the body.","q":"Which gyrus is the primary motor cortex, and which is the primary sensory cortex?","a":"Precentral gyrus: motor. Postcentral gyrus: sensory."},{"id":"kcn8","ch":"s15","title":"Gray outside in the cerebrum","core":"The cerebrum needs huge amounts of gray matter (cell bodies that do the thinking) in a small skull, so it puts it outside as a folded cortex to maximize surface area, with white matter tracts underneath. The spinal cord is a cable, so it is the reverse: white tracts outside, gray inside.","trap":"Memorizing the two layouts without the reason. Cortex = surface area for computing; cord = room for long cables.","q":"The cord keeps its gray matter inside. Why does the cerebrum do the opposite?","a":"A folded outer cortex packs the most gray matter (thinking cells) into the skull; the cord's job is long cables, so its white matter is outside."},{"id":"kcn9","ch":"s15","title":"The diencephalon","core":"The thalamus is the relay station for nearly every sensory pathway on its way to the cortex. The hypothalamus, below it, controls temperature, hunger, thirst and sleep, and the pituitary through the infundibulum. The pineal gland, under the back of the corpus callosum, secretes melatonin.","trap":"Confusing thalamus and hypothalamus. Thalamus relays sensation; hypothalamus runs homeostasis and the pituitary.","q":"Which structure relays nearly all sensory input to the cortex? Which one controls the pituitary?","a":"Thalamus relays sensation. The hypothalamus controls the pituitary through the infundibulum."},{"id":"kcn10","ch":"s15","title":"Brainstem and cerebellum","core":"Top to bottom: midbrain (corpora quadrigemina: superior colliculi for vision, inferior colliculi for hearing; cerebral aqueduct), pons (the bridge relaying cortex to cerebellum), medulla oblongata (heart rate, blood pressure, breathing; pyramids carry the corticospinal tract, which crosses at the pyramidal decussation). The cerebellum corrects movements already underway; its white matter tree is the arbor vitae, and it acts on the same side of the body.","trap":"Thinking the cerebellum starts movement. It corrects movement the cortex has already started.","q":"Which brainstem part houses the cardiovascular and respiratory centers?","a":"The medulla oblongata."},{"id":"kcn11","ch":"s15","title":"The midsagittal core and limbic structures","core":"The corpus callosum is the thick arched band of white matter joining the hemispheres (front: genu; back: splenium). The fornix, a thinner arch beneath it, belongs to the limbic system. The septum pellucidum is the thin sheet between the lateral ventricles. The amygdala drives fear and emotional memory, the hippocampus forms new memories, and the mammillary bodies connect to the hippocampus through the fornix. Most tracts decussate, so each hemisphere handles the opposite side of the body.","trap":"Calling the fornix a commissure. The corpus callosum connects the hemispheres; the fornix is a limbic pathway.","q":"What connects the two cerebral hemispheres? What might be lost if it were cut?","a":"The corpus callosum. Cut it, and the two hemispheres can no longer share information directly."},{"id":"kcn12","ch":"s15","title":"Ventricles and the CSF path","core":"Choroid plexus makes CSF. Path: lateral ventricles, interventricular foramen, third ventricle, cerebral aqueduct, fourth ventricle, subarachnoid space, arachnoid granulations, dural venous sinuses. A block anywhere backs fluid up: hydrocephalus. In a newborn the skull bones are not yet fused, so the head itself enlarges.","trap":"Saying CSF is made in the subarachnoid space or reabsorbed by the choroid plexus. Made by choroid plexus, reabsorbed at arachnoid granulations.","q":"Where is CSF made, and where is it reabsorbed?","a":"Made by the choroid plexus in the ventricles; reabsorbed at the arachnoid granulations into the dural venous sinuses."},{"id":"kcn13","ch":"s16","title":"The twelve cranial nerves","core":"Twelve pairs, numbered I to XII from front to back along the brain: I olfactory, II optic, III oculomotor, IV trochlear, V trigeminal, VI abducens, VII facial, VIII vestibulocochlear, IX glossopharyngeal, X vagus, XI accessory, XII hypoglossal. Purely sensory: I, II, VIII. Purely motor: III, IV, VI, XI, XII. Both: V, VII, IX, X.","trap":"Learning names without numbers. Exams and charts use both, often just the Roman numeral.","q":"How many pairs of cranial nerves are there, and how are they numbered?","a":"Twelve pairs, numbered I to XII (Roman numerals) from front to back."},{"id":"kcn14","ch":"s16","title":"Trigeminal vs facial","core":"The trigeminal (V) is sensory for the face and motor for chewing; its three divisions are ophthalmic (V1), maxillary (V2) and mandibular (V3). The facial (VII) runs the muscles of facial expression and carries taste from the front two thirds of the tongue, plus tears and saliva.","trap":"Confusing them because both live in the face. Feeling the face = V. Moving the face = VII.","q":"Which cranial nerve moves the muscles of facial expression, and which one carries facial sensation?","a":"Facial expression: VII, the facial nerve. Facial sensation and chewing: V, the trigeminal."},{"id":"kcn15","ch":"s16","title":"The eye-movement nerves","core":"\"LR6 SO4, rest 3\": the lateral rectus is supplied by VI (abducens), the superior oblique by IV (trochlear), and the other four extrinsic eye muscles by III (oculomotor), which also raises the eyelid and constricts the pupil.","trap":"Thinking each eye muscle has its own nerve. Three nerves cover six muscles.","q":"Use the \"LR6 SO4, rest 3\" rule: which nerve moves each eye muscle?","a":"Lateral rectus: VI (abducens). Superior oblique: IV (trochlear). The other four: III (oculomotor)."},{"id":"kcn16","ch":"s16","title":"The wanderer, the tongue and the throat","core":"The vagus (X) is the only cranial nerve that wanders far below the head and neck: it carries parasympathetic fibers to the heart, lungs and most of the digestive tract, plus swallowing and voice. The hypoglossal (XII) moves the tongue. The glossopharyngeal (IX) carries taste from the back third of the tongue and helps with swallowing. The accessory (XI) runs the trapezius and sternocleidomastoid.","trap":"Assigning tongue movement to the glossopharyngeal because of \"glosso.\" Tongue MOVEMENT is XII; IX is taste from the back of the tongue.","q":"Why does the vagus reach so far beyond the head and neck?","a":"It carries the parasympathetic supply to the heart, lungs and most of the digestive tract."},{"id":"kcn17","ch":"s16","title":"Sheep brain vs human brain","core":"Four differences from the Lab 16 slide. The sheep has huge olfactory bulbs (it relies more on smell); ours are small. The sheep has ONE fused mammillary body; we have two side by side. The sheep's interthalamic adhesion is large; ours is small and missing in about 1 in 4 people. The sheep's brainstem runs straight back (four legs); ours bends straight down (upright). On the ventral surface the reliable roots are I (olfactory bulbs), II (optic chiasm), V beside the pons, VI near the midline, and VII with VIII where the pons meets the cerebellum.","trap":"Labeling the sheep drawing from a human diagram. Label what the sheep actually shows.","q":"Name two ways a sheep brain differs from a human brain.","a":"Any two: huge olfactory bulbs, one fused mammillary body, a large interthalamic adhesion, a brainstem that runs straight back."},{"id":"kcn18","ch":"s17","title":"Thirty-one pairs of spinal nerves","core":"31 pairs: 8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, 1 coccygeal. There are 8 cervical nerves but only 7 cervical vertebrae, because the first nerve leaves above C1.","trap":"Assuming one nerve per vertebra, so 7 cervical. There are 8 cervical nerve pairs.","q":"How many pairs of spinal nerves are there, and how many are cervical?","a":"31 pairs; 8 are cervical."},{"id":"kcn19","ch":"s17","title":"The four plexuses","core":"Ventral rami of neighboring spinal nerves weave into plexuses. Cervical plexus (C1 to C4, with C5): the phrenic nerve to the diaphragm. Brachial plexus (C5 to T1): the arm and hand. Lumbar plexus (L1 to L4): femoral and obturator nerves of the thigh. Sacral plexus (L4 to S4): the sciatic nerve.","trap":"Putting the phrenic nerve in the brachial plexus. It comes from the cervical plexus (C3, 4, 5 keep the diaphragm alive).","q":"Which plexus gives rise to the phrenic nerve, and what does it supply?","a":"The cervical plexus. The phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm."},{"id":"kcn20","ch":"s17","title":"The brachial plexus, five terminal branches","core":"Musculocutaneous (front of the arm: biceps, brachialis), axillary (deltoid, teres minor), radial (back of the arm and forearm: triceps and the extensors), median (most forearm flexors and the thumb muscles; through the carpal tunnel), ulnar (medial hand and most small hand muscles; the \"funny bone\").","trap":"Mixing up median and ulnar. Median = middle of the wrist, carpal tunnel. Ulnar = little-finger side, funny bone.","q":"The brachial plexus ends in five named nerves. List them.","a":"Musculocutaneous, axillary, radial, median, ulnar."},{"id":"kcn21","ch":"s17","title":"Nerves of the lower limb","core":"Femoral nerve (lumbar plexus): front of the thigh, the quadriceps. Obturator nerve (lumbar plexus): medial thigh, the adductors. Sciatic nerve (sacral plexus): the largest nerve in the body, to the back of the thigh, splitting just above the knee into the tibial and common fibular nerves.","trap":"Forgetting the obturator, tibial and common fibular nerves when listing the eleven named nerves.","q":"What is the largest nerve in the body, which plexus does it come from, and what does it split into?","a":"The sciatic nerve, from the sacral plexus. It splits into the tibial and common fibular nerves."},{"id":"kcn22","ch":"s17","title":"Dermatomes","core":"A dermatome is the strip of skin supplied by one spinal nerve root. Maps of them let a clinician localize nerve damage from what a patient can and cannot feel. Landmarks: C6 = thumb, C7 = middle finger, C8 = ring and little finger.","trap":"Confusing C6, C7 and C8 in the hand. Thumb 6, middle 7, little 8.","q":"What is a dermatome, and why are dermatome maps useful?","a":"The skin area supplied by one spinal nerve root. Maps localize nerve or root damage from a patient's sensory loss."},{"id":"kcn23","ch":"s17","title":"The reflex arc","core":"Five parts in order: receptor, sensory neuron, integration center (gray matter of the cord), motor neuron, effector. The patellar (knee-jerk) stretch reflex is monosynaptic: the sensory neuron synapses directly on the motor neuron, no interneuron, so it is fast (about 25 ms). The withdrawal reflex adds interneurons (polysynaptic) to coordinate several muscles.","trap":"Tapping the kneecap instead of the patellar tendon. Bone does not stretch; the tendon tap stretches the quadriceps.","q":"What makes the knee-jerk reflex monosynaptic?","a":"There is exactly one synapse, sensory neuron directly onto motor neuron, with no interneuron."},{"id":"kcn24","ch":"s18","title":"Two autonomic divisions","core":"Sympathetic: thoracolumbar outflow (T1 to L2), fight or flight. Parasympathetic: craniosacral outflow (cranial nerves III, VII, IX, X plus S2 to S4), rest and digest. Most organs get both (dual innervation) and are tuned up or down; a few get sympathetic only (most blood vessels, sweat glands, arrector pili, the adrenal medulla).","trap":"Swapping the outflow names. Thoracolumbar = sympathetic; craniosacral = parasympathetic.","q":"Which division uses thoracolumbar outflow, and which uses craniosacral outflow?","a":"Thoracolumbar: sympathetic. Craniosacral: parasympathetic."},{"id":"kcn25","ch":"s18","title":"Ganglion position explains the whole personality","core":"Sympathetic ganglia sit close to the cord (the chain beside it, or prevertebral ganglia): short preganglionic fibers, long postganglionic fibers, and the chain spreads one input to many organs at once, so the response is body-wide. Parasympathetic ganglia sit at or in the target organ: long preganglionic, short postganglionic, one organ at a time.","trap":"Thinking the sympathetic division feels widespread because it is \"stronger.\" It is the ganglion geometry.","q":"Why does sympathetic activation feel like it affects the whole body at once?","a":"Its ganglia sit near the cord, and the chain lets one preganglionic fiber reach many postganglionic fibers headed to many organs."},{"id":"kcn26","ch":"s18","title":"Transmitters and the adrenal medulla","core":"Every preganglionic fiber releases acetylcholine onto nicotinic receptors. Parasympathetic postganglionic fibers release acetylcholine onto muscarinic receptors. Sympathetic postganglionic fibers release norepinephrine onto adrenergic receptors, except at sweat glands (acetylcholine). The adrenal medulla is a modified sympathetic ganglion: its chromaffin cells release epinephrine and norepinephrine into the blood as hormones.","trap":"Saying the adrenal medulla is just a gland with its own nerve. It IS a sympathetic ganglion whose cells release into blood.","q":"What is the adrenal medulla, in nervous-system terms?","a":"A modified sympathetic ganglion: chromaffin cells (postganglionic neurons without axons) release epinephrine and norepinephrine into the blood."},{"id":"kcn27","ch":"s18","title":"Somatic vs autonomic motor pathways","core":"Somatic: one motor neuron from the CNS straight to skeletal muscle, acetylcholine, voluntary. Autonomic: two neurons in a chain with a ganglion between them, to smooth muscle, cardiac muscle and glands, involuntary.","trap":"Forgetting the ganglion. A two-neuron chain with a ganglion is the autonomic signature.","q":"Looking only at the wiring, how can you tell an autonomic motor pathway from a somatic one?","a":"Somatic: one neuron, no ganglion, to skeletal muscle. Autonomic: two neurons with a ganglion, to smooth muscle, cardiac muscle and glands."},{"id":"kcn28","ch":"s18","title":"Receptors and adaptation","core":"Transduction turns a stimulus into an electrical signal. Tonic receptors adapt slowly and keep firing (pain); phasic receptors fire at the start and go quiet (touch, vibration: why you stop feeling your shirt). Six categories: chemoreceptors, thermoreceptors, photoreceptors, mechanoreceptors, baroreceptors (a kind of mechanoreceptor for vessel stretch) and nociceptors. Two-point discrimination measures how densely touch receptors are packed.","trap":"Calling baroreceptors a separate sense. They are mechanoreceptors that sense stretch in a vessel wall.","q":"What is the difference between tonic and phasic receptors?","a":"Tonic: adapt slowly and keep firing (pain). Phasic: fire at the start, then go quiet (touch)."},{"id":"kcn29","ch":"s18","title":"Taste and smell","core":"Five tastes: sweet, sour, salty, bitter, umami. There is no tongue map; every taste is detected across the tongue. Taste buds sit in fungiform, foliate and vallate papillae, not filiform. Taste from the front two thirds travels on VII, the back third on IX. Olfactory receptor axons pass through the cribriform plate of the ethmoid to the olfactory bulb, and smell is the one sense that reaches the cortex without the thalamus.","trap":"Believing the tongue map. Thresholds vary a little; location does not decide taste.","q":"Name the five primary tastes.","a":"Sweet, sour, salty, bitter and umami."},{"id":"kcn30","ch":"s19","title":"The three tunics of the eye","core":"Fibrous tunic (outer): sclera, the tough white wall, and cornea, the clear window. Vascular tunic (middle): choroid, ciliary body and iris. Neural tunic (inner): retina, with the photoreceptors.","trap":"Putting the iris in the fibrous tunic because it is at the front. It is part of the vascular tunic.","q":"Name the three tunics of the eye from outside in, with one structure in each.","a":"Fibrous (sclera, cornea), vascular (choroid, ciliary body, iris), neural (retina)."},{"id":"kcn31","ch":"s19","title":"Inside the eye","core":"The lens, held by ciliary zonules, changes shape to focus (accommodation). In front of the lens, the anterior segment holds aqueous humor, which drains through the canal of Schlemm; block it and pressure rises: glaucoma. Behind the lens, the vitreous humor is made once, before birth, and never replaced. The optic disc has no photoreceptors because it is where the ganglion cell axons leave as the optic nerve and the vessels enter: the blind spot. The ora serrata is the jagged front edge of the retina.","trap":"Thinking the blind spot is a damaged area. It is normal anatomy: the exit point of the optic nerve.","q":"Why does the optic disc have no photoreceptors?","a":"It is where the axons leave the eye as the optic nerve and where the retinal vessels enter, so there is no room for receptors."},{"id":"kcn32","ch":"s19","title":"Eye muscles, lids and tears","core":"Six extrinsic muscles: four recti (superior, inferior, medial, lateral) and two obliques. The lacrimal gland sits superior and lateral in the orbit; tears cross the eye and drain medially into the lacrimal sac and down the nasolacrimal duct into the nose, which is why crying gives you a runny nose. The palpebrae are the eyelids.","trap":"Putting the lacrimal gland in the inner corner. The gland is upper outer; the drainage is the inner corner.","q":"Trace tears from where they are made to where they drain.","a":"Lacrimal gland (upper outer orbit), across the eye, lacrimal sac (inner corner), nasolacrimal duct, nasal cavity."},{"id":"kcn33","ch":"s19","title":"The ear, outer to inner","core":"Outer ear: auricle and external acoustic meatus, ending at the tympanic membrane. Middle ear (air-filled): the ossicles malleus, incus and stapes carry vibration from the eardrum to the oval window; the round window releases the pressure. Inner ear (fluid-filled): cochlea (hearing), vestibule (utricle and saccule) and three semicircular canals.","trap":"Putting the ossicles in the inner ear. They are in the air-filled middle ear.","q":"Sound hits the eardrum. Which tiny bones pass it inward, in what order, and why are they there?","a":"Malleus, incus, stapes. They carry and amplify the eardrum's vibration to the oval window."},{"id":"kcn34","ch":"s19","title":"Hearing and balance receptors","core":"The organ of Corti sits on the basilar membrane; vibration moves the membrane and bends hair cells against the tectorial membrane. The utricle and saccule sense gravity and straight-line movement (head tilt, an elevator); the three semicircular canals sense rotation. Cochlear hair cells do not regenerate, so noise-induced hearing loss is permanent.","trap":"Swapping vestibule and canals. Canals = turning; utricle and saccule = tilt and straight-line motion.","q":"What do the semicircular canals detect, and why is noise damage permanent?","a":"Rotation of the head. Noise kills cochlear hair cells, and they do not grow back."},{"id":"kc28","ch":"s13","title":"Parts of a neuron","core":"Dendrites receive signals; the cell body (soma) holds the nucleus; the axon hillock is where the neuron decides whether to fire; the axon carries the impulse away from the cell body; the myelin sheath insulates it, with gaps called nodes of Ranvier; axon terminals release the signal to the next cell.","trap":"Sending the signal from the axon toward the dendrites. It runs dendrites, cell body, axon hillock, axon, terminals.","q":"Where does a neuron decide whether to fire?","a":"At the axon hillock, where the axon leaves the cell body."},{"id":"kc31","ch":"s13","title":"The six neuroglia","core":"CNS (4): astrocytes (support, blood-brain barrier, scar after injury), oligodendrocytes (CNS myelin), microglia (phagocytes that clean up debris and pathogens), ependymal cells (line the ventricles and central canal, help make CSF). PNS (2): Schwann cells (PNS myelin), satellite cells (surround cell bodies in ganglia).","trap":"Putting satellite cells in the CNS. They sit around cell bodies in PNS ganglia.","q":"Name the four CNS neuroglia and the two PNS neuroglia.","a":"CNS: astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, microglia, ependymal cells. PNS: Schwann cells, satellite cells."},{"id":"kc32","ch":"s13","title":"Classifying neurons","core":"By structure: multipolar (many dendrites, one axon; motor neurons and interneurons), bipolar (one dendrite, one axon; retina, olfactory epithelium), pseudounipolar (one process that splits; sensory neurons with cell bodies in dorsal root ganglia), anaxonic (no distinct axon; brain, retina). By function: sensory, motor, interneuron.","trap":"Calling sensory neurons multipolar. Most are pseudounipolar.","q":"What structural type are most sensory neurons, and most motor neurons?","a":"Sensory: pseudounipolar. Motor: multipolar."}],"mcq":[{"kc":"kcn1","q":"Below about L2, what fills the vertebral canal in an adult?","c":["The spinal cord itself","The cauda equina: nerve roots floating in CSF","Only fat","The brainstem"],"a":1,"why":"The cord ends at L1 to L2; below it the lumbar and sacral roots run down as the cauda equina, bathed in CSF.","no":["The cord has already ended by then.","","Fat is in the epidural space, but the canal also holds roots and CSF.","The brainstem is in the skull."],"id":"q1"},{"kc":"kcn1","q":"What is the tapering end of the spinal cord called?","c":["Filum terminale","Conus medullaris","Cauda equina","Central canal"],"a":1,"why":"The cord tapers to the conus medullaris around L1 to L2.","no":["The filum terminale is the thread of pia that anchors the conus to the coccyx.","","The cauda equina is the bundle of roots below the conus.","The central canal runs inside the cord."],"id":"q2"},{"kc":"kcn1","q":"Why is a lumbar puncture done between L3 and L4 rather than at T10?","c":["The CSF is thicker lower down","Below L2 the needle enters fluid around floating roots, with no cord to hit","The dura is missing below L2","There is no subarachnoid space at T10"],"a":1,"why":"The cord ends around L1 to L2, so lower down the needle meets only roots that slide out of the way.","no":["CSF is the same everywhere.","","The dura continues down to about S2.","The subarachnoid space surrounds the whole cord."],"id":"q3"},{"kc":"kcn2","q":"A patient loses pain and touch from one area but can still move it. Which part of the cord gray matter is most likely damaged?","c":["Ventral horn","Dorsal horn","Lateral horn","Gray commissure only"],"a":1,"why":"Dorsal horns receive incoming sensory signals; the ventral horns, which send motor commands, are spared.","no":["Ventral horn damage would cause weakness.","","Lateral horns hold autonomic cell bodies.","The commissure connects the two sides around the central canal."],"id":"q4"},{"kc":"kcn2","q":"Which segments of the cord have lateral horns, and what do they hold?","c":["C1 to C8; motor neurons to the arm","T1 to L2; sympathetic (autonomic) cell bodies","All segments; sensory ganglia","S2 to S4; the cauda equina"],"a":1,"why":"Lateral horns appear from T1 to L2 and hold the cell bodies of sympathetic preganglionic neurons.","no":["Arm motor neurons are in the ventral horns.","","Sensory ganglia sit outside the cord, on the dorsal roots.","S2 to S4 carry parasympathetic outflow, and the cauda equina is below the cord."],"id":"q5"},{"kc":"kcn2","q":"What are the white matter columns of the cord made of?","c":["Neuron cell bodies","Myelinated axons running up and down the cord","Cerebrospinal fluid","Dorsal root ganglia"],"a":1,"why":"The columns (funiculi) are tracts of myelinated axons: the cable between brain and body.","no":["Cell bodies are in the gray matter.","","CSF is in the central canal and subarachnoid space.","Dorsal root ganglia sit outside the cord."],"id":"q6"},{"kc":"kcn3","q":"An anesthetic for labor is injected outside the dura, into a space with fat and vessels. Which space is that?","c":["Subarachnoid space","Epidural space","Central canal","Fourth ventricle"],"a":1,"why":"The epidural space lies outside the dura and holds fat and vessels; that is where an epidural goes.","no":["The subarachnoid space holds CSF, inside the arachnoid.","","The central canal is inside the cord.","The fourth ventricle is in the brain."],"id":"q7"},{"kc":"kcn3","q":"Which meninx hugs the surface of the brain and cord directly?","c":["Dura mater","Arachnoid mater","Pia mater","Periosteum"],"a":2,"why":"The pia mater is the delicate innermost layer, following every groove.","no":["The dura is the tough outer layer.","The arachnoid is the web-like middle layer.","","Periosteum covers bone, not the CNS."],"id":"q8"},{"kc":"kcn4","q":"Which branch of a spinal nerve goes on to form the plexuses?","c":["Dorsal ramus","Ventral ramus","Dorsal root","Gray commissure"],"a":1,"why":"The ventral rami weave together into the cervical, brachial, lumbar and sacral plexuses.","no":["The dorsal ramus serves the skin and muscles of the back.","","The dorsal root carries sensory fibers into the cord.","The commissure is inside the cord."],"id":"q9"},{"kc":"kcn4","q":"A spinal nerve, just after the roots join, carries:","c":["Only sensory fibers","Only motor fibers","Both sensory and motor fibers","Only autonomic fibers"],"a":2,"why":"Dorsal (sensory) and ventral (motor) roots merge, so the spinal nerve is mixed.","no":["That is the dorsal root alone.","That is the ventral root alone.","","It carries somatic fibers too."],"id":"q10"},{"kc":"kcn5","q":"Which layer wraps a single fascicle of axons inside a nerve?","c":["Endoneurium","Perineurium","Epineurium","Myelin sheath"],"a":1,"why":"Perineurium goes around each fascicle.","no":["Endoneurium wraps each individual axon.","","Epineurium wraps the whole nerve.","Myelin insulates individual axons; it is not connective tissue."],"id":"q11"},{"kc":"kcn6","q":"Which groove separates the frontal lobe from the parietal lobe?","c":["Lateral sulcus","Central sulcus","Longitudinal fissure","Transverse fissure"],"a":1,"why":"The central sulcus divides frontal from parietal; motor cortex is just in front of it, sensory cortex just behind.","no":["The lateral sulcus sets off the temporal lobe.","","The longitudinal fissure separates the two hemispheres.","The transverse fissure separates cerebrum from cerebellum."],"id":"q12"},{"kc":"kcn6","q":"Which lobe is hidden deep inside the lateral sulcus?","c":["Occipital","Insula","Temporal","Parietal"],"a":1,"why":"The insula is buried beneath the frontal, parietal and temporal lobes; it handles taste and visceral sensation.","no":["The occipital lobe is at the back, on the surface.","","The temporal lobe is visible on the side.","The parietal lobe is on top, behind the central sulcus."],"id":"q13"},{"kc":"kcn7","q":"A stroke damages the left precentral gyrus. What would you expect?","c":["Loss of feeling on the left side","Weakness on the right side of the body","Loss of vision","Loss of hearing"],"a":1,"why":"The precentral gyrus is primary motor cortex, and motor tracts cross, so the left side controls the right body.","no":["Feeling is the postcentral gyrus, and it would also be the opposite side.","","Vision is the occipital lobe.","Hearing is the temporal lobe."],"id":"q14"},{"kc":"kcn8","q":"Why is the cerebral cortex folded into gyri and sulci?","c":["To protect it from blows","To fit far more gray matter surface into the skull","To make room for CSF","To separate the two hemispheres"],"a":1,"why":"Folding packs a huge surface of cortex, the thinking gray matter, into a limited skull.","no":["The skull, meninges and CSF do the protecting.","","CSF has its own spaces.","The longitudinal fissure separates the hemispheres."],"id":"q15"},{"kc":"kcn9","q":"Which brain structure secretes melatonin?","c":["Hypothalamus","Pineal gland","Thalamus","Pituitary"],"a":1,"why":"The pineal gland, under the back of the corpus callosum, secretes melatonin.","no":["The hypothalamus controls the pituitary and homeostasis.","","The thalamus relays sensory signals.","The pituitary hangs below the hypothalamus and makes other hormones."],"id":"q16"},{"kc":"kcn9","q":"What connects the hypothalamus to the pituitary gland?","c":["Fornix","Infundibulum","Cerebral aqueduct","Corpus callosum"],"a":1,"why":"The infundibulum is the stalk from the hypothalamus to the pituitary.","no":["The fornix is a limbic pathway.","","The aqueduct carries CSF.","The corpus callosum connects the hemispheres."],"id":"q17"},{"kc":"kcn10","q":"Which midbrain structures handle visual and auditory reflexes?","c":["Cerebral peduncles","Corpora quadrigemina (superior and inferior colliculi)","Pyramids","Mammillary bodies"],"a":1,"why":"The superior colliculi handle vision and the inferior colliculi hearing.","no":["The cerebral peduncles carry motor tracts down the front of the midbrain.","","The pyramids carry motor tracts on the medulla.","The mammillary bodies are part of the limbic circuit."],"id":"q18"},{"kc":"kcn10","q":"Where do the corticospinal (motor) tracts cross to the opposite side?","c":["In the pons","At the pyramidal decussation in the medulla","In the thalamus","In the cerebellum"],"a":1,"why":"The pyramids of the medulla carry the corticospinal tract, which crosses at the pyramidal decussation.","no":["The pons relays to the cerebellum.","","The thalamus relays sensation.","The cerebellum acts on the same side."],"id":"q19"},{"kc":"kcn10","q":"A cerebellar tumor on the right side would most likely make which side of the body clumsy?","c":["The left side","The right side","Both sides equally","Neither; the cerebellum only affects eye movement"],"a":1,"why":"The cerebellum's outflow crosses twice, so it acts on the same (ipsilateral) side.","no":["That is the pattern for the cerebrum, not the cerebellum.","","One side is affected.","The cerebellum coordinates all body movement."],"id":"q20"},{"kc":"kcn11","q":"What is the front, curved end of the corpus callosum called?","c":["Splenium","Genu","Fornix","Septum pellucidum"],"a":1,"why":"Genu means knee: the front bend. The splenium is the thick back end.","no":["The splenium is the back end.","","The fornix is a separate arch beneath it.","The septum pellucidum is the thin sheet between the lateral ventricles."],"id":"q21"},{"kc":"kcn11","q":"Which limbic structure drives fear and emotional memory?","c":["Hippocampus","Amygdala","Mammillary body","Pineal gland"],"a":1,"why":"The amygdala drives fear and emotional memory.","no":["The hippocampus forms new memories.","","The mammillary bodies connect to the hippocampus.","The pineal gland secretes melatonin."],"id":"q22"},{"kc":"kcn12","q":"CSF leaves the third ventricle for the fourth ventricle through the:","c":["Interventricular foramen","Cerebral aqueduct","Central canal","Arachnoid granulations"],"a":1,"why":"The cerebral aqueduct runs through the midbrain from the third to the fourth ventricle.","no":["The interventricular foramen links the lateral ventricles to the third.","","The central canal runs down the cord.","Arachnoid granulations reabsorb CSF."],"id":"q23"},{"kc":"kcn12","q":"A tumor squeezes the cerebral aqueduct shut. Which ventricles swell?","c":["Only the fourth","The lateral and third ventricles","None; CSF just reroutes","Only the central canal"],"a":1,"why":"Fluid backs up behind the block: the lateral and third ventricles enlarge (hydrocephalus).","no":["The fourth is downstream of the block.","","There is no detour around the aqueduct.","The central canal is downstream too."],"id":"q24"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which three cranial nerves are purely sensory?","c":["I, II, VIII","III, IV, VI","V, VII, IX","X, XI, XII"],"a":0,"why":"Olfactory (I), optic (II) and vestibulocochlear (VIII) carry only sensory signals.","no":["","III, IV and VI are motor (eye movement).","V, VII and IX are mixed.","X is mixed; XI and XII are motor."],"id":"q25"},{"kc":"kcn14","q":"A patient cannot feel a cotton swab on the forehead or upper eyelid, but the cheek and jaw feel normal. Which trigeminal division is affected?","c":["Ophthalmic (V1)","Maxillary (V2)","Mandibular (V3)","The facial nerve (VII)"],"a":0,"why":"V1 ophthalmic covers the forehead and eye, V2 maxillary the cheek and upper teeth, V3 mandibular the jaw, lower teeth and chewing muscles.","no":["","V2 covers the cheek, which feels normal.","V3 covers the jaw, which feels normal.","VII moves the face; it does not carry facial touch."],"id":"q26"},{"kc":"kcn15","q":"A patient's right eye cannot turn outward, away from the nose. Which nerve is most likely affected?","c":["Trochlear (IV)","Abducens (VI)","Oculomotor (III)","Optic (II)"],"a":1,"why":"Turning the eye outward is the lateral rectus, supplied by VI, the abducens (it ABducts).","no":["IV runs the superior oblique.","","III runs the other four muscles, not the lateral rectus.","II carries vision, not movement."],"id":"q27"},{"kc":"kcn16","q":"Asked to stick out the tongue, a patient's tongue moves poorly. Which cranial nerve is most likely involved?","c":["Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Facial (VII)","Vagus (X)"],"a":1,"why":"The hypoglossal nerve moves the tongue.","no":["IX carries taste from the back of the tongue and helps swallowing.","","VII moves the face and carries taste from the front of the tongue.","X handles swallowing, voice and parasympathetic supply."],"id":"q28"},{"kc":"kcn17","q":"Which pair of features would you find on a sheep brain but not on a human one?","c":["Small olfactory bulbs and two mammillary bodies","Huge olfactory bulbs and one fused mammillary body","No interthalamic adhesion at all","A brainstem that bends straight down"],"a":1,"why":"A sheep relies heavily on smell, and its mammillary bodies are fused into one.","no":["Those are the human features.","","The sheep's adhesion is large; it is the human one that can be missing.","That is the upright human brainstem; the sheep's runs straight back."],"id":"q29"},{"kc":"kcn17","q":"Why does a sheep's brainstem run straight back instead of bending down like ours?","c":["Sheep have no cerebellum","A sheep walks on four legs, so its spinal cord runs horizontally","The sheep brainstem is shorter","It is bent during the dissection"],"a":1,"why":"A four-legged animal's cord runs horizontally behind the head; an upright human's runs straight down.","no":["Sheep have a cerebellum.","","Length is not the reason.","It runs straight back in life, not because of handling."],"id":"q30"},{"kc":"kcn18","q":"How many pairs of thoracic spinal nerves are there?","c":["7","8","12","5"],"a":2,"why":"Twelve thoracic pairs, one for each thoracic vertebra.","no":["7 is the number of cervical vertebrae.","8 is the number of cervical nerve pairs.","","5 is lumbar or sacral."],"id":"q31"},{"kc":"kcn19","q":"A patient breaks the neck at C2 and cannot breathe without a ventilator. Why?","c":["The vagus nerve is cut","The injury is above the C3 to C5 roots of the phrenic nerve to the diaphragm","The brachial plexus is torn","The medulla is crushed"],"a":1,"why":"The phrenic nerve comes from C3 to C5 (cervical plexus). An injury above it cuts the diaphragm off from the brain.","no":["The vagus does not drive the diaphragm.","","The brachial plexus serves the arm.","A C2 injury is in the cord, below the medulla."],"id":"q32"},{"kc":"kcn20","q":"Numbness in the thumb, index and middle fingers that is worse at night, from pressure at the wrist, points to which nerve?","c":["Ulnar","Median","Radial","Axillary"],"a":1,"why":"Carpal tunnel syndrome: the median nerve is squeezed as it passes through the carpal tunnel.","no":["The ulnar nerve serves the little-finger side.","","The radial nerve serves the back of the arm and forearm.","The axillary nerve serves the deltoid."],"id":"q33"},{"kc":"kcn20","q":"Hitting the \"funny bone\" at the elbow tingles the little finger. Which nerve was struck?","c":["Median","Ulnar","Radial","Musculocutaneous"],"a":1,"why":"The ulnar nerve runs behind the medial epicondyle with little padding.","no":["The median nerve runs down the front of the forearm.","","The radial nerve runs around the back of the humerus.","The musculocutaneous nerve is in the front of the arm."],"id":"q34"},{"kc":"kcn20","q":"After a fracture of the humeral shaft, a patient cannot extend the wrist (\"wrist drop\"). Which nerve is injured?","c":["Radial","Ulnar","Median","Axillary"],"a":0,"why":"The radial nerve runs in the radial groove of the humerus and supplies the wrist and finger extensors.","no":["","The ulnar nerve serves the medial hand.","The median nerve serves the flexors.","The axillary nerve serves the deltoid near the shoulder."],"id":"q35"},{"kc":"kcn21","q":"A patient cannot extend the knee after an injury in the front of the thigh. Which nerve is most likely damaged?","c":["Obturator","Femoral","Tibial","Common fibular"],"a":1,"why":"The femoral nerve supplies the quadriceps, which extend the knee.","no":["The obturator nerve supplies the adductors.","","The tibial nerve serves the back of the leg.","The common fibular nerve serves the front and side of the leg."],"id":"q36"},{"kc":"kcn21","q":"A patient's foot drops and drags because they cannot dorsiflex. Which nerve is most likely injured?","c":["Tibial","Common fibular","Femoral","Obturator"],"a":1,"why":"The common fibular nerve supplies the dorsiflexors in the front of the leg; it wraps around the fibular neck, where it is easily injured.","no":["The tibial nerve supplies the plantar flexors.","","The femoral nerve supplies the quadriceps.","The obturator supplies the adductors."],"id":"q37"},{"kc":"kcn22","q":"Which spinal nerve root supplies the skin of the thumb?","c":["C5","C6","C7","C8"],"a":1,"why":"Thumb = C6. Middle finger = C7. Ring and little finger = C8.","no":["C5 covers the shoulder and lateral arm.","","C7 is the middle finger.","C8 is the ring and little finger."],"id":"q38"},{"kc":"kcn23","q":"In the reflex arc, where does the integration happen?","c":["In the receptor","In the gray matter of the spinal cord","In the effector muscle","In the cerebral cortex"],"a":1,"why":"The integration center is in the cord's gray matter, which is why reflexes work without the brain.","no":["The receptor detects the stimulus.","","The effector carries out the response.","The brain is not needed for a spinal reflex."],"id":"q39"},{"kc":"kcn23","q":"Why is the withdrawal reflex slower than the knee jerk?","c":["It uses the brain","It has interneurons, so more synapses","Its axons are unmyelinated","It uses the autonomic division"],"a":1,"why":"The withdrawal reflex is polysynaptic: interneurons coordinate several muscles, and each synapse adds delay.","no":["Both are spinal reflexes.","","Myelination is not the difference here.","Both use somatic motor neurons."],"id":"q40"},{"kc":"kcn24","q":"Which cranial nerves carry parasympathetic outflow?","c":["III, VII, IX, X","I, II, VIII","IV, VI, XI","V, XII"],"a":0,"why":"The cranial part of craniosacral outflow travels in III, VII, IX and X.","no":["","These are purely sensory.","These are somatic motor nerves.","These carry no parasympathetic fibers."],"id":"q41"},{"kc":"kcn24","q":"Which target gets sympathetic input only, with no parasympathetic supply?","c":["The heart","Sweat glands","The stomach","The pupil"],"a":1,"why":"Sweat glands, most blood vessels, arrector pili and the adrenal medulla are sympathetic only.","no":["The heart has dual innervation.","","The digestive tract has dual innervation.","The pupil has dual innervation."],"id":"q42"},{"kc":"kcn25","q":"In which division is the preganglionic fiber long and the postganglionic fiber short?","c":["Sympathetic","Parasympathetic","Somatic","Both autonomic divisions equally"],"a":1,"why":"Parasympathetic ganglia are at or in the target organ, so the first fiber travels the long distance.","no":["Sympathetic preganglionic fibers are short.","","Somatic pathways have no ganglion.","The two divisions are opposite."],"id":"q43"},{"kc":"kcn26","q":"What do postganglionic parasympathetic fibers release at their targets?","c":["Norepinephrine onto adrenergic receptors","Acetylcholine onto muscarinic receptors","Epinephrine into the blood","Acetylcholine onto nicotinic receptors at a skeletal muscle"],"a":1,"why":"Parasympathetic postganglionic fibers are cholinergic: ACh on muscarinic receptors.","no":["That is most sympathetic postganglionic fibers.","","That is the adrenal medulla.","That is the somatic neuromuscular junction."],"id":"q44"},{"kc":"kcn27","q":"Which pathway reaches its target with a single neuron from the CNS?","c":["Somatic motor to skeletal muscle","Sympathetic to a sweat gland","Parasympathetic to the heart","Sympathetic to a blood vessel"],"a":0,"why":"Somatic motor neurons run straight from the CNS to skeletal muscle; autonomic pathways use two neurons with a ganglion.","no":["","Two neurons with a ganglion.","Two neurons with a ganglion.","Two neurons with a ganglion."],"id":"q45"},{"kc":"kcn28","q":"Why do you stop feeling your watch on your wrist a minute after putting it on?","c":["Pain receptors take over","Phasic touch receptors adapt and go quiet","The nerve is damaged","The thalamus blocks all touch"],"a":1,"why":"Phasic receptors fire at the start of a stimulus and then adapt; they report change, not steady pressure.","no":["Pain receptors are tonic and are not being triggered.","","Nothing is damaged.","The thalamus relays touch normally."],"id":"q46"},{"kc":"kcn28","q":"Two caliper points feel like two at the fingertip but like one on the back. What does that show?","c":["The back has more touch receptors","Touch receptors are packed far more densely in the fingertip","The back has no sensory nerves","The fingertip is warmer"],"a":1,"why":"Two-point discrimination measures receptor density; fingertips are densely supplied.","no":["It is the reverse.","","The back has sensory nerves, just fewer receptors per area.","Temperature is not the reason."],"id":"q47"},{"kc":"kcn29","q":"Which tongue papillae carry no taste buds?","c":["Vallate","Fungiform","Foliate","Filiform"],"a":3,"why":"Filiform papillae give the tongue its rough texture but have no taste buds.","no":["Vallate papillae have many taste buds.","Fungiform papillae have taste buds.","Foliate papillae have taste buds.",""],"id":"q48"},{"kc":"kcn29","q":"Olfactory receptor axons pass through which bony structure on their way to the olfactory bulb?","c":["Sella turcica","Cribriform plate of the ethmoid","Foramen magnum","Optic canal"],"a":1,"why":"They pass through the small holes of the cribriform plate.","no":["The sella turcica holds the pituitary.","","The foramen magnum passes the spinal cord.","The optic canal passes the optic nerve."],"id":"q49"},{"kc":"kcn30","q":"The iris belongs to which tunic of the eye?","c":["Fibrous tunic","Vascular tunic","Neural tunic","None; it floats in the aqueous humor"],"a":1,"why":"The vascular tunic is the choroid, the ciliary body and the iris.","no":["The fibrous tunic is the sclera and cornea.","","The neural tunic is the retina.","It is attached at the ciliary body as part of the vascular tunic."],"id":"q50"},{"kc":"kcn31","q":"Aqueous humor cannot drain, and pressure inside the eye climbs. Which drainage route is blocked, and what is the condition?","c":["Nasolacrimal duct; dry eye","Canal of Schlemm (scleral venous sinus); glaucoma","Optic disc; blind spot","Ciliary zonules; cataract"],"a":1,"why":"Aqueous humor drains through the canal of Schlemm; a block raises pressure and can damage the optic nerve (glaucoma).","no":["The nasolacrimal duct drains tears, outside the eyeball.","","The optic disc is the nerve exit.","Zonules hold the lens; a cataract is a cloudy lens."],"id":"q51"},{"kc":"kcn31","q":"What is accommodation?","c":["The pupil getting smaller in bright light","The lens changing shape to focus at different distances","The retina adapting to the dark","Tears spreading across the cornea"],"a":1,"why":"The lens, held by the ciliary zonules, thickens for near objects and flattens for far ones.","no":["That is the pupillary light reflex.","","That is dark adaptation.","That is the lacrimal system."],"id":"q52"},{"kc":"kcn32","q":"Why does crying give you a runny nose?","c":["Tears are made in the nose","Tears drain through the lacrimal sac and nasolacrimal duct into the nasal cavity","The facial nerve also controls the nose","Crying raises blood pressure"],"a":1,"why":"Tears drain medially into the lacrimal sac and down the nasolacrimal duct into the nose.","no":["Tears are made in the lacrimal gland in the orbit.","","This is plumbing, not nerve supply.","Pressure is not the cause."],"id":"q53"},{"kc":"kcn33","q":"The stapes pushes on which structure to move the fluid of the inner ear?","c":["Round window","Oval window","Tympanic membrane","Auditory tube"],"a":1,"why":"The footplate of the stapes sits in the oval window; the round window bulges to release the pressure.","no":["The round window releases pressure; it is not pushed by the stapes.","","The eardrum is at the start of the chain.","The auditory tube connects the middle ear to the throat."],"id":"q54"},{"kc":"kcn34","q":"On which membrane does the organ of Corti sit?","c":["Tectorial membrane","Basilar membrane","Tympanic membrane","Vestibular membrane"],"a":1,"why":"The organ of Corti rests on the basilar membrane; its hair cells bend against the tectorial membrane above them.","no":["The tectorial membrane lies over the hair cells.","","The tympanic membrane is the eardrum.","The vestibular membrane is the roof of the cochlear duct."],"id":"q55"},{"kc":"kcn34","q":"Riding an elevator that starts moving up, which receptors tell you?","c":["Semicircular canals","Utricle and saccule","Organ of Corti","Olfactory epithelium"],"a":1,"why":"The utricle and saccule sense gravity and straight-line acceleration.","no":["The canals sense rotation.","","The organ of Corti senses sound.","The olfactory epithelium senses smell."],"id":"q56"},{"kc":"kc28","q":"Which parts of a neuron receive incoming signals from other cells?","c":["Dendrites","Axon terminals","Myelin sheath","Nodes of Ranvier"],"a":0,"why":"Dendrites are the receiving branches; they carry signals toward the cell body.","no":["","Axon terminals SEND the signal on to the next cell.","Myelin insulates the axon.","Nodes are gaps in the myelin along the axon."],"id":"q57"},{"kc":"kc28","q":"What are the nodes of Ranvier?","c":["Branches of dendrites","Gaps in the myelin sheath along the axon","Clusters of cell bodies","Vesicles in the axon terminal"],"a":1,"why":"Nodes of Ranvier are the bare gaps between myelin segments.","no":["Dendrites branch from the cell body.","","Clusters of cell bodies are ganglia or nuclei.","Vesicles hold neurotransmitter in the terminals."],"id":"q58"},{"kc":"kc31","q":"Which neuroglia line the ventricles and help make cerebrospinal fluid?","c":["Astrocytes","Microglia","Ependymal cells","Satellite cells"],"a":2,"why":"Ependymal cells line the ventricles and central canal.","no":["Astrocytes support neurons and the blood-brain barrier.","Microglia are the CNS phagocytes.","","Satellite cells are in PNS ganglia."],"id":"q59"},{"kc":"kc31","q":"Which neuroglia surround neuron cell bodies inside ganglia?","c":["Satellite cells","Schwann cells","Oligodendrocytes","Ependymal cells"],"a":0,"why":"Satellite cells surround cell bodies in PNS ganglia.","no":["","Schwann cells wrap axons.","Oligodendrocytes are CNS.","Ependymal cells line ventricles."],"id":"q60"},{"kc":"kc32","q":"A sensory neuron has one process that splits into two branches, with its cell body off to the side in a dorsal root ganglion. Its structural type is:","c":["Multipolar","Bipolar","Pseudounipolar","Anaxonic"],"a":2,"why":"One process that splits, cell body off to the side: pseudounipolar, typical of sensory neurons.","no":["Multipolar has many dendrites and one axon; motor neurons are multipolar.","Bipolar has two separate processes, as in the retina.","","Anaxonic has no distinct axon."],"id":"q61"},{"kc":"kc32","q":"Which neurons are bipolar?","c":["Motor neurons in the spinal cord","Neurons of the retina and olfactory epithelium","Most sensory neurons of the skin","Interneurons of the cortex"],"a":1,"why":"Bipolar neurons, with one dendrite and one axon, are found in special sense organs like the retina and olfactory epithelium.","no":["Motor neurons are multipolar.","","Skin sensory neurons are pseudounipolar.","Most interneurons are multipolar."],"id":"q62"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve is number I?","c":["Olfactory","Trigeminal","Accessory","Vagus"],"a":0,"why":"Cranial nerve I is the olfactory nerve: smell.","no":["","That is cranial nerve V.","That is cranial nerve XI.","That is cranial nerve X."],"id":"q63"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"What number is the olfactory nerve?","c":["I","IV","VII","IX"],"a":0,"why":"The olfactory nerve is cranial nerve I.","no":["","IV is the trochlear nerve.","VII is the facial nerve.","IX is the glossopharyngeal nerve."],"id":"q64"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve handles smell?","c":["I, olfactory","XII, hypoglossal","XI, accessory","III, oculomotor"],"a":0,"why":"The olfactory nerve (I) handles smell.","no":["","The hypoglossal nerve handles movement of the tongue.","The accessory nerve handles the trapezius and sternocleidomastoid.","The oculomotor nerve handles four of the six eye muscles, lifting the eyelid and constricting the pupil."],"id":"q65"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve is number II?","c":["Optic","Accessory","Olfactory","Glossopharyngeal"],"a":0,"why":"Cranial nerve II is the optic nerve: vision.","no":["","That is cranial nerve XI.","That is cranial nerve I.","That is cranial nerve IX."],"id":"q66"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"What number is the optic nerve?","c":["II","VI","X","V"],"a":0,"why":"The optic nerve is cranial nerve II.","no":["","VI is the abducens nerve.","X is the vagus nerve.","V is the trigeminal nerve."],"id":"q67"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve handles vision?","c":["II, optic","V, trigeminal","IX, glossopharyngeal","X, vagus"],"a":0,"why":"The optic nerve (II) handles vision.","no":["","The trigeminal nerve handles facial sensation and chewing.","The glossopharyngeal nerve handles taste from the back third of the tongue and swallowing.","The vagus nerve handles parasympathetic supply to the heart, lungs and digestive tract, plus swallowing and voice."],"id":"q68"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve is number III?","c":["Oculomotor","Vagus","Glossopharyngeal","Vestibulocochlear"],"a":0,"why":"Cranial nerve III is the oculomotor nerve: four of the six eye muscles, lifting the eyelid and constricting the pupil.","no":["","That is cranial nerve X.","That is cranial nerve IX.","That is cranial nerve VIII."],"id":"q69"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"What number is the oculomotor nerve?","c":["III","XII","IV","V"],"a":0,"why":"The oculomotor nerve is cranial nerve III.","no":["","XII is the hypoglossal nerve.","IV is the trochlear nerve.","V is the trigeminal nerve."],"id":"q70"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve handles four of the six eye muscles, lifting the eyelid and constricting the pupil?","c":["III, oculomotor","XII, hypoglossal","IV, trochlear","X, vagus"],"a":0,"why":"The oculomotor nerve (III) handles four of the six eye muscles, lifting the eyelid and constricting the pupil.","no":["","The hypoglossal nerve handles movement of the tongue.","The trochlear nerve handles the superior oblique muscle of the eye.","The vagus nerve handles parasympathetic supply to the heart, lungs and digestive tract, plus swallowing and voice."],"id":"q71"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve is number IV?","c":["Trochlear","Vestibulocochlear","Olfactory","Optic"],"a":0,"why":"Cranial nerve IV is the trochlear nerve: the superior oblique muscle of the eye.","no":["","That is cranial nerve VIII.","That is cranial nerve I.","That is cranial nerve II."],"id":"q72"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"What number is the trochlear nerve?","c":["IV","III","XI","I"],"a":0,"why":"The trochlear nerve is cranial nerve IV.","no":["","III is the oculomotor nerve.","XI is the accessory nerve.","I is the olfactory nerve."],"id":"q73"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve handles the superior oblique muscle of the eye?","c":["IV, trochlear","VI, abducens","I, olfactory","XII, hypoglossal"],"a":0,"why":"The trochlear nerve (IV) handles the superior oblique muscle of the eye.","no":["","The abducens nerve handles the lateral rectus, turning the eye outward.","The olfactory nerve handles smell.","The hypoglossal nerve handles movement of the tongue."],"id":"q74"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve is number V?","c":["Trigeminal","Glossopharyngeal","Accessory","Vestibulocochlear"],"a":0,"why":"Cranial nerve V is the trigeminal nerve: facial sensation and chewing.","no":["","That is cranial nerve IX.","That is cranial nerve XI.","That is cranial nerve VIII."],"id":"q75"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"What number is the trigeminal nerve?","c":["V","VIII","XII","IX"],"a":0,"why":"The trigeminal nerve is cranial nerve V.","no":["","VIII is the vestibulocochlear nerve.","XII is the hypoglossal nerve.","IX is the glossopharyngeal nerve."],"id":"q76"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve handles facial sensation and chewing?","c":["V, trigeminal","III, oculomotor","VII, facial","II, optic"],"a":0,"why":"The trigeminal nerve (V) handles facial sensation and chewing.","no":["","The oculomotor nerve handles four of the six eye muscles, lifting the eyelid and constricting the pupil.","The facial nerve handles facial expression, taste from the front two thirds of the tongue, tears and saliva.","The optic nerve handles vision."],"id":"q77"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve is number VI?","c":["Abducens","Olfactory","Oculomotor","Glossopharyngeal"],"a":0,"why":"Cranial nerve VI is the abducens nerve: the lateral rectus, turning the eye outward.","no":["","That is cranial nerve I.","That is cranial nerve III.","That is cranial nerve IX."],"id":"q78"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"What number is the abducens nerve?","c":["VI","IV","V","VIII"],"a":0,"why":"The abducens nerve is cranial nerve VI.","no":["","IV is the trochlear nerve.","V is the trigeminal nerve.","VIII is the vestibulocochlear nerve."],"id":"q79"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve handles the lateral rectus, turning the eye outward?","c":["VI, abducens","XII, hypoglossal","V, trigeminal","VIII, vestibulocochlear"],"a":0,"why":"The abducens nerve (VI) handles the lateral rectus, turning the eye outward.","no":["","The hypoglossal nerve handles movement of the tongue.","The trigeminal nerve handles facial sensation and chewing.","The vestibulocochlear nerve handles hearing and balance."],"id":"q80"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve is number VII?","c":["Facial","Vagus","Vestibulocochlear","Abducens"],"a":0,"why":"Cranial nerve VII is the facial nerve: facial expression, taste from the front two thirds of the tongue, tears and saliva.","no":["","That is cranial nerve X.","That is cranial nerve VIII.","That is cranial nerve VI."],"id":"q81"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"What number is the facial nerve?","c":["VII","X","XI","VIII"],"a":0,"why":"The facial nerve is cranial nerve VII.","no":["","X is the vagus nerve.","XI is the accessory nerve.","VIII is the vestibulocochlear nerve."],"id":"q82"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve handles facial expression, taste from the front two thirds of the tongue, tears and saliva?","c":["VII, facial","XI, accessory","IV, trochlear","VI, abducens"],"a":0,"why":"The facial nerve (VII) handles facial expression, taste from the front two thirds of the tongue, tears and saliva.","no":["","The accessory nerve handles the trapezius and sternocleidomastoid.","The trochlear nerve handles the superior oblique muscle of the eye.","The abducens nerve handles the lateral rectus, turning the eye outward."],"id":"q83"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve is number VIII?","c":["Vestibulocochlear","Hypoglossal","Olfactory","Trigeminal"],"a":0,"why":"Cranial nerve VIII is the vestibulocochlear nerve: hearing and balance.","no":["","That is cranial nerve XII.","That is cranial nerve I.","That is cranial nerve V."],"id":"q84"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"What number is the vestibulocochlear nerve?","c":["VIII","XI","III","VI"],"a":0,"why":"The vestibulocochlear nerve is cranial nerve VIII.","no":["","XI is the accessory nerve.","III is the oculomotor nerve.","VI is the abducens nerve."],"id":"q85"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve handles hearing and balance?","c":["VIII, vestibulocochlear","X, vagus","XI, accessory","II, optic"],"a":0,"why":"The vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII) handles hearing and balance.","no":["","The vagus nerve handles parasympathetic supply to the heart, lungs and digestive tract, plus swallowing and voice.","The accessory nerve handles the trapezius and sternocleidomastoid.","The optic nerve handles vision."],"id":"q86"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve is number IX?","c":["Glossopharyngeal","Hypoglossal","Trochlear","Trigeminal"],"a":0,"why":"Cranial nerve IX is the glossopharyngeal nerve: taste from the back third of the tongue and swallowing.","no":["","That is cranial nerve XII.","That is cranial nerve IV.","That is cranial nerve V."],"id":"q87"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"What number is the glossopharyngeal nerve?","c":["IX","V","II","XI"],"a":0,"why":"The glossopharyngeal nerve is cranial nerve IX.","no":["","V is the trigeminal nerve.","II is the optic nerve.","XI is the accessory nerve."],"id":"q88"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve handles taste from the back third of the tongue and swallowing?","c":["IX, glossopharyngeal","VIII, vestibulocochlear","XII, hypoglossal","II, optic"],"a":0,"why":"The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) handles taste from the back third of the tongue and swallowing.","no":["","The vestibulocochlear nerve handles hearing and balance.","The hypoglossal nerve handles movement of the tongue.","The optic nerve handles vision."],"id":"q89"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve is number X?","c":["Vagus","Abducens","Optic","Facial"],"a":0,"why":"Cranial nerve X is the vagus nerve: parasympathetic supply to the heart, lungs and digestive tract, plus swallowing and voice.","no":["","That is cranial nerve VI.","That is cranial nerve II.","That is cranial nerve VII."],"id":"q90"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"What number is the vagus nerve?","c":["X","III","I","V"],"a":0,"why":"The vagus nerve is cranial nerve X.","no":["","III is the oculomotor nerve.","I is the olfactory nerve.","V is the trigeminal nerve."],"id":"q91"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve handles parasympathetic supply to the heart, lungs and digestive tract, plus swallowing and voice?","c":["X, vagus","VII, facial","XII, hypoglossal","II, optic"],"a":0,"why":"The vagus nerve (X) handles parasympathetic supply to the heart, lungs and digestive tract, plus swallowing and voice.","no":["","The facial nerve handles facial expression, taste from the front two thirds of the tongue, tears and saliva.","The hypoglossal nerve handles movement of the tongue.","The optic nerve handles vision."],"id":"q92"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve is number XI?","c":["Accessory","Olfactory","Vagus","Hypoglossal"],"a":0,"why":"Cranial nerve XI is the accessory nerve: the trapezius and sternocleidomastoid.","no":["","That is cranial nerve I.","That is cranial nerve X.","That is cranial nerve XII."],"id":"q93"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"What number is the accessory nerve?","c":["XI","VII","X","VI"],"a":0,"why":"The accessory nerve is cranial nerve XI.","no":["","VII is the facial nerve.","X is the vagus nerve.","VI is the abducens nerve."],"id":"q94"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve handles the trapezius and sternocleidomastoid?","c":["XI, accessory","IX, glossopharyngeal","V, trigeminal","XII, hypoglossal"],"a":0,"why":"The accessory nerve (XI) handles the trapezius and sternocleidomastoid.","no":["","The glossopharyngeal nerve handles taste from the back third of the tongue and swallowing.","The trigeminal nerve handles facial sensation and chewing.","The hypoglossal nerve handles movement of the tongue."],"id":"q95"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve is number XII?","c":["Hypoglossal","Trochlear","Olfactory","Trigeminal"],"a":0,"why":"Cranial nerve XII is the hypoglossal nerve: movement of the tongue.","no":["","That is cranial nerve IV.","That is cranial nerve I.","That is cranial nerve V."],"id":"q96"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"What number is the hypoglossal nerve?","c":["XII","I","II","X"],"a":0,"why":"The hypoglossal nerve is cranial nerve XII.","no":["","I is the olfactory nerve.","II is the optic nerve.","X is the vagus nerve."],"id":"q97"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which cranial nerve handles movement of the tongue?","c":["XII, hypoglossal","X, vagus","IX, glossopharyngeal","I, olfactory"],"a":0,"why":"The hypoglossal nerve (XII) handles movement of the tongue.","no":["","The vagus nerve handles parasympathetic supply to the heart, lungs and digestive tract, plus swallowing and voice.","The glossopharyngeal nerve handles taste from the back third of the tongue and swallowing.","The olfactory nerve handles smell."],"id":"q98"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which of these cranial nerves is purely sensory?","c":["I, olfactory","VI, abducens","X, vagus","V, trigeminal"],"a":0,"why":"The olfactory nerve (I) is purely sensory.","no":["","The abducens nerve is purely motor.","The vagus nerve is mixed.","The trigeminal nerve is mixed."],"id":"q99"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which of these cranial nerves is purely motor?","c":["III, oculomotor","VIII, vestibulocochlear","V, trigeminal","II, optic"],"a":0,"why":"The oculomotor nerve (III) is purely motor.","no":["","The vestibulocochlear nerve is purely sensory.","The trigeminal nerve is mixed.","The optic nerve is purely sensory."],"id":"q100"},{"kc":"kcn13","q":"Which of these cranial nerves is mixed (both sensory and motor)?","c":["V, trigeminal","I, olfactory","XI, accessory","III, oculomotor"],"a":0,"why":"The trigeminal nerve (V) is mixed (both sensory and motor).","no":["","The olfactory nerve is purely sensory.","The accessory nerve is purely motor.","The oculomotor nerve is purely motor."],"id":"q101"},{"kc":"kcn19","q":"Which nerve supplies the diaphragm?","c":["Phrenic","Femoral","Common fibular","Radial"],"a":0,"why":"The phrenic nerve (cervical plexus) supplies the diaphragm.","no":["","The femoral nerve supplies the front of the thigh, the quadriceps.","The common fibular nerve supplies the front and side of the leg, the dorsiflexors.","The radial nerve supplies the back of the arm and forearm, the extensors."],"id":"q102"},{"kc":"kcn19","q":"Trace the phrenic nerve back toward the spine. Which plexus is it a branch of?","c":["Cervical plexus","Brachial plexus","Lumbar plexus","Sacral plexus"],"a":0,"why":"The phrenic nerve arises from the cervical plexus.","no":["","The brachial plexus does not give rise to the phrenic nerve.","The lumbar plexus does not give rise to the phrenic nerve.","The sacral plexus does not give rise to the phrenic nerve."],"id":"q103"},{"kc":"kcn20","q":"Which nerve supplies the front of the arm, including biceps brachii?","c":["Musculocutaneous","Obturator","Common fibular","Sciatic"],"a":0,"why":"The musculocutaneous nerve (brachial plexus) supplies the front of the arm, including biceps brachii.","no":["","The obturator nerve supplies the medial thigh, the adductors.","The common fibular nerve supplies the front and side of the leg, the dorsiflexors.","The sciatic nerve supplies the back of the thigh, then the leg through its two branches."],"id":"q104"},{"kc":"kcn19","q":"Trace the musculocutaneous nerve back toward the spine. Which plexus is it a branch of?","c":["Brachial plexus","Cervical plexus","Lumbar plexus","Sacral plexus"],"a":0,"why":"The musculocutaneous nerve arises from the brachial plexus.","no":["","The cervical plexus does not give rise to the musculocutaneous nerve.","The lumbar plexus does not give rise to the musculocutaneous nerve.","The sacral plexus does not give rise to the musculocutaneous nerve."],"id":"q105"},{"kc":"kcn20","q":"Which nerve supplies the deltoid and teres minor?","c":["Axillary","Tibial","Obturator","Musculocutaneous"],"a":0,"why":"The axillary nerve (brachial plexus) supplies the deltoid and teres minor.","no":["","The tibial nerve supplies the back of the leg and the plantar flexors.","The obturator nerve supplies the medial thigh, the adductors.","The musculocutaneous nerve supplies the front of the arm, including biceps brachii."],"id":"q106"},{"kc":"kcn19","q":"Trace the axillary nerve back toward the spine. Which plexus is it a branch of?","c":["Brachial plexus","Cervical plexus","Lumbar plexus","Sacral plexus"],"a":0,"why":"The axillary nerve arises from the brachial plexus.","no":["","The cervical plexus does not give rise to the axillary nerve.","The lumbar plexus does not give rise to the axillary nerve.","The sacral plexus does not give rise to the axillary nerve."],"id":"q107"},{"kc":"kcn20","q":"Which nerve supplies the back of the arm and forearm, the extensors?","c":["Radial","Common fibular","Tibial","Ulnar"],"a":0,"why":"The radial nerve (brachial plexus) supplies the back of the arm and forearm, the extensors.","no":["","The common fibular nerve supplies the front and side of the leg, the dorsiflexors.","The tibial nerve supplies the back of the leg and the plantar flexors.","The ulnar nerve supplies the little-finger side of the hand and most small hand muscles."],"id":"q108"},{"kc":"kcn19","q":"Trace the radial nerve back toward the spine. Which plexus is it a branch of?","c":["Brachial plexus","Cervical plexus","Lumbar plexus","Sacral plexus"],"a":0,"why":"The radial nerve arises from the brachial plexus.","no":["","The cervical plexus does not give rise to the radial nerve.","The lumbar plexus does not give rise to the radial nerve.","The sacral plexus does not give rise to the radial nerve."],"id":"q109"},{"kc":"kcn20","q":"Which nerve supplies most forearm flexors and the thumb muscles, through the carpal tunnel?","c":["Median","Obturator","Radial","Ulnar"],"a":0,"why":"The median nerve (brachial plexus) supplies most forearm flexors and the thumb muscles, through the carpal tunnel.","no":["","The obturator nerve supplies the medial thigh, the adductors.","The radial nerve supplies the back of the arm and forearm, the extensors.","The ulnar nerve supplies the little-finger side of the hand and most small hand muscles."],"id":"q110"},{"kc":"kcn19","q":"Trace the median nerve back toward the spine. Which plexus is it a branch of?","c":["Brachial plexus","Cervical plexus","Lumbar plexus","Sacral plexus"],"a":0,"why":"The median nerve arises from the brachial plexus.","no":["","The cervical plexus does not give rise to the median nerve.","The lumbar plexus does not give rise to the median nerve.","The sacral plexus does not give rise to the median nerve."],"id":"q111"},{"kc":"kcn20","q":"Which nerve supplies the little-finger side of the hand and most small hand muscles?","c":["Ulnar","Obturator","Median","Tibial"],"a":0,"why":"The ulnar nerve (brachial plexus) supplies the little-finger side of the hand and most small hand muscles.","no":["","The obturator nerve supplies the medial thigh, the adductors.","The median nerve supplies most forearm flexors and the thumb muscles, through the carpal tunnel.","The tibial nerve supplies the back of the leg and the plantar flexors."],"id":"q112"},{"kc":"kcn19","q":"Trace the ulnar nerve back toward the spine. Which plexus is it a branch of?","c":["Brachial plexus","Cervical plexus","Lumbar plexus","Sacral plexus"],"a":0,"why":"The ulnar nerve arises from the brachial plexus.","no":["","The cervical plexus does not give rise to the ulnar nerve.","The lumbar plexus does not give rise to the ulnar nerve.","The sacral plexus does not give rise to the ulnar nerve."],"id":"q113"},{"kc":"kcn21","q":"Which nerve supplies the front of the thigh, the quadriceps?","c":["Femoral","Tibial","Ulnar","Phrenic"],"a":0,"why":"The femoral nerve (lumbar plexus) supplies the front of the thigh, the quadriceps.","no":["","The tibial nerve supplies the back of the leg and the plantar flexors.","The ulnar nerve supplies the little-finger side of the hand and most small hand muscles.","The phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm."],"id":"q114"},{"kc":"kcn19","q":"Trace the femoral nerve back toward the spine. Which plexus is it a branch of?","c":["Lumbar plexus","Cervical plexus","Brachial plexus","Sacral plexus"],"a":0,"why":"The femoral nerve arises from the lumbar plexus.","no":["","The cervical plexus does not give rise to the femoral nerve.","The brachial plexus does not give rise to the femoral nerve.","The sacral plexus does not give rise to the femoral nerve."],"id":"q115"},{"kc":"kcn21","q":"Which nerve supplies the medial thigh, the adductors?","c":["Obturator","Femoral","Ulnar","Phrenic"],"a":0,"why":"The obturator nerve (lumbar plexus) supplies the medial thigh, the adductors.","no":["","The femoral nerve supplies the front of the thigh, the quadriceps.","The ulnar nerve supplies the little-finger side of the hand and most small hand muscles.","The phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm."],"id":"q116"},{"kc":"kcn19","q":"Trace the obturator nerve back toward the spine. Which plexus is it a branch of?","c":["Lumbar plexus","Cervical plexus","Brachial plexus","Sacral plexus"],"a":0,"why":"The obturator nerve arises from the lumbar plexus.","no":["","The cervical plexus does not give rise to the obturator nerve.","The brachial plexus does not give rise to the obturator nerve.","The sacral plexus does not give rise to the obturator nerve."],"id":"q117"},{"kc":"kcn21","q":"Which nerve supplies the back of the thigh, then the leg through its two branches?","c":["Sciatic","Femoral","Median","Obturator"],"a":0,"why":"The sciatic nerve (sacral plexus) supplies the back of the thigh, then the leg through its two branches.","no":["","The femoral nerve supplies the front of the thigh, the quadriceps.","The median nerve supplies most forearm flexors and the thumb muscles, through the carpal tunnel.","The obturator nerve supplies the medial thigh, the adductors."],"id":"q118"},{"kc":"kcn19","q":"Trace the sciatic nerve back toward the spine. Which plexus is it a branch of?","c":["Sacral plexus","Cervical plexus","Brachial plexus","Lumbar plexus"],"a":0,"why":"The sciatic nerve arises from the sacral plexus.","no":["","The cervical plexus does not give rise to the sciatic nerve.","The brachial plexus does not give rise to the sciatic nerve.","The lumbar plexus does not give rise to the sciatic nerve."],"id":"q119"},{"kc":"kcn21","q":"Which nerve supplies the back of the leg and the plantar flexors?","c":["Tibial","Axillary","Phrenic","Ulnar"],"a":0,"why":"The tibial nerve (sacral plexus) supplies the back of the leg and the plantar flexors.","no":["","The axillary nerve supplies the deltoid and teres minor.","The phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm.","The ulnar nerve supplies the little-finger side of the hand and most small hand muscles."],"id":"q120"},{"kc":"kcn19","q":"Trace the tibial nerve back toward the spine. Which plexus is it a branch of?","c":["Sacral plexus","Cervical plexus","Brachial plexus","Lumbar plexus"],"a":0,"why":"The tibial nerve arises from the sacral plexus.","no":["","The cervical plexus does not give rise to the tibial nerve.","The brachial plexus does not give rise to the tibial nerve.","The lumbar plexus does not give rise to the tibial nerve."],"id":"q121"},{"kc":"kcn21","q":"Which nerve supplies the front and side of the leg, the dorsiflexors?","c":["Common fibular","Ulnar","Radial","Axillary"],"a":0,"why":"The common fibular nerve (sacral plexus) supplies the front and side of the leg, the dorsiflexors.","no":["","The ulnar nerve supplies the little-finger side of the hand and most small hand muscles.","The radial nerve supplies the back of the arm and forearm, the extensors.","The axillary nerve supplies the deltoid and teres minor."],"id":"q122"},{"kc":"kcn19","q":"Trace the common fibular nerve back toward the spine. Which plexus is it a branch of?","c":["Sacral plexus","Cervical plexus","Brachial plexus","Lumbar plexus"],"a":0,"why":"The common fibular nerve arises from the sacral plexus.","no":["","The cervical plexus does not give rise to the common fibular nerve.","The brachial plexus does not give rise to the common fibular nerve.","The lumbar plexus does not give rise to the common fibular nerve."],"id":"q123"},{"kc":"kcn9","q":"Which of these relays nearly all sensory input to the cerebral cortex?","c":["Thalamus","Choroid plexus","Inferior colliculi","Amygdala"],"a":0,"why":"The thalamus relays nearly all sensory input to the cerebral cortex.","no":["","The choroid plexus produces cerebrospinal fluid.","The inferior colliculi handle auditory reflexes.","The amygdala drives fear and emotional memory."],"id":"q124"},{"kc":"kcn9","q":"Which of these controls body temperature, hunger, thirst and the pituitary?","c":["Hypothalamus","Arachnoid granulations","Superior colliculi","Insula"],"a":0,"why":"The hypothalamus controls body temperature, hunger, thirst and the pituitary.","no":["","The arachnoid granulations return cerebrospinal fluid to the venous blood.","The superior colliculi handle visual reflexes.","The insula handles taste and visceral sensation."],"id":"q125"},{"kc":"kcn9","q":"Which of these secretes melatonin?","c":["Pineal gland","Corpus callosum","Thalamus","Amygdala"],"a":0,"why":"The pineal gland secretes melatonin.","no":["","The corpus callosum joins the two cerebral hemispheres.","The thalamus relays nearly all sensory input to the cerebral cortex.","The amygdala drives fear and emotional memory."],"id":"q126"},{"kc":"kcn10","q":"Which of these houses the heart rate, blood pressure and breathing centers?","c":["Medulla oblongata","Thalamus","Choroid plexus","Insula"],"a":0,"why":"The medulla oblongata houses the heart rate, blood pressure and breathing centers.","no":["","The thalamus relays nearly all sensory input to the cerebral cortex.","The choroid plexus produces cerebrospinal fluid.","The insula handles taste and visceral sensation."],"id":"q127"},{"kc":"kcn10","q":"Which of these relays signals between the cortex and the cerebellum?","c":["Pons","Inferior colliculi","Superior colliculi","Choroid plexus"],"a":0,"why":"The pons relays signals between the cortex and the cerebellum.","no":["","The inferior colliculi handle auditory reflexes.","The superior colliculi handle visual reflexes.","The choroid plexus produces cerebrospinal fluid."],"id":"q128"},{"kc":"kcn10","q":"Which of these handle visual reflexes?","c":["Superior colliculi","Corpus callosum","Pons","Amygdala"],"a":0,"why":"The superior colliculi handle visual reflexes.","no":["","The corpus callosum joins the two cerebral hemispheres.","The pons relays signals between the cortex and the cerebellum.","The amygdala drives fear and emotional memory."],"id":"q129"},{"kc":"kcn10","q":"Which of these handle auditory reflexes?","c":["Inferior colliculi","Amygdala","Superior colliculi","Pineal gland"],"a":0,"why":"The inferior colliculi handle auditory reflexes.","no":["","The amygdala drives fear and emotional memory.","The superior colliculi handle visual reflexes.","The pineal gland secretes melatonin."],"id":"q130"},{"kc":"kcn10","q":"Which of these corrects movements already underway?","c":["Cerebellum","Superior colliculi","Pineal gland","Insula"],"a":0,"why":"The cerebellum corrects movements already underway.","no":["","The superior colliculi handle visual reflexes.","The pineal gland secretes melatonin.","The insula handles taste and visceral sensation."],"id":"q131"},{"kc":"kcn11","q":"Which of these joins the two cerebral hemispheres?","c":["Corpus callosum","Precentral gyrus","Superior colliculi","Amygdala"],"a":0,"why":"The corpus callosum joins the two cerebral hemispheres.","no":["","The precentral gyrus is the primary motor cortex.","The superior colliculi handle visual reflexes.","The amygdala drives fear and emotional memory."],"id":"q132"},{"kc":"kcn11","q":"Which of these forms new memories?","c":["Hippocampus","Pons","Insula","Precentral gyrus"],"a":0,"why":"The hippocampus forms new memories.","no":["","The pons relays signals between the cortex and the cerebellum.","The insula handles taste and visceral sensation.","The precentral gyrus is the primary motor cortex."],"id":"q133"},{"kc":"kcn11","q":"Which of these drives fear and emotional memory?","c":["Amygdala","Inferior colliculi","Hypothalamus","Precentral gyrus"],"a":0,"why":"The amygdala drives fear and emotional memory.","no":["","The inferior colliculi handle auditory reflexes.","The hypothalamus controls body temperature, hunger, thirst and the pituitary.","The precentral gyrus is the primary motor cortex."],"id":"q134"},{"kc":"kcn12","q":"Which of these produces cerebrospinal fluid?","c":["Choroid plexus","Postcentral gyrus","Thalamus","Hippocampus"],"a":0,"why":"The choroid plexus produces cerebrospinal fluid.","no":["","The postcentral gyrus is the primary sensory cortex.","The thalamus relays nearly all sensory input to the cerebral cortex.","The hippocampus forms new memories."],"id":"q135"},{"kc":"kcn12","q":"Which of these return cerebrospinal fluid to the venous blood?","c":["Arachnoid granulations","Amygdala","Choroid plexus","Pineal gland"],"a":0,"why":"The arachnoid granulations return cerebrospinal fluid to the venous blood.","no":["","The amygdala drives fear and emotional memory.","The choroid plexus produces cerebrospinal fluid.","The pineal gland secretes melatonin."],"id":"q136"},{"kc":"kcn7","q":"Which of these is the primary motor cortex?","c":["Precentral gyrus","Pons","Corpus callosum","Medulla oblongata"],"a":0,"why":"The precentral gyrus is the primary motor cortex.","no":["","The pons relays signals between the cortex and the cerebellum.","The corpus callosum joins the two cerebral hemispheres.","The medulla oblongata houses the heart rate, blood pressure and breathing centers."],"id":"q137"},{"kc":"kcn7","q":"Which of these is the primary sensory cortex?","c":["Postcentral gyrus","Amygdala","Arachnoid granulations","Pons"],"a":0,"why":"The postcentral gyrus is the primary sensory cortex.","no":["","The amygdala drives fear and emotional memory.","The arachnoid granulations return cerebrospinal fluid to the venous blood.","The pons relays signals between the cortex and the cerebellum."],"id":"q138"},{"kc":"kcn6","q":"Which of these handles taste and visceral sensation?","c":["Insula","Medulla oblongata","Superior colliculi","Pineal gland"],"a":0,"why":"The insula handles taste and visceral sensation.","no":["","The medulla oblongata houses the heart rate, blood pressure and breathing centers.","The superior colliculi handle visual reflexes.","The pineal gland secretes melatonin."],"id":"q139"},{"kc":"kcn30","q":"Which of these is the clear front window of the fibrous tunic?","c":["Cornea","Semicircular canals","Stapes","Round window"],"a":0,"why":"The cornea is the clear front window of the fibrous tunic.","no":["","The semicircular canals sense rotation of the head.","The stapes pushes on the oval window.","The round window bulges to release pressure from the cochlea."],"id":"q140"},{"kc":"kcn30","q":"Which of these is the tough white outer wall of the eye?","c":["Sclera","Retina","Choroid","Tympanic membrane"],"a":0,"why":"The sclera is the tough white outer wall of the eye.","no":["","The retina holds the photoreceptors.","The choroid is the dark vascular layer under the sclera.","The tympanic membrane vibrates with sound at the end of the ear canal."],"id":"q141"},{"kc":"kcn30","q":"Which of these is the dark vascular layer under the sclera?","c":["Choroid","Stapes","Vitreous humor","Cochlea"],"a":0,"why":"The choroid is the dark vascular layer under the sclera.","no":["","The stapes pushes on the oval window.","The vitreous humor fills the space behind the lens and is never replaced.","The cochlea is the spiral organ of hearing."],"id":"q142"},{"kc":"kcn31","q":"Which of these holds the zonules and makes aqueous humor?","c":["Ciliary body","Lacrimal gland","Cochlea","Basilar membrane"],"a":0,"why":"The ciliary body holds the zonules and makes aqueous humor.","no":["","The lacrimal gland makes tears.","The cochlea is the spiral organ of hearing.","The basilar membrane is the membrane the organ of Corti sits on."],"id":"q143"},{"kc":"kcn30","q":"Which of these holds the photoreceptors?","c":["Retina","Vitreous humor","Sclera","Cochlea"],"a":0,"why":"The retina holds the photoreceptors.","no":["","The vitreous humor fills the space behind the lens and is never replaced.","The sclera is the tough white outer wall of the eye.","The cochlea is the spiral organ of hearing."],"id":"q144"},{"kc":"kcn31","q":"Which of these changes shape to focus light?","c":["Lens","Optic disc","Utricle and saccule","Tympanic membrane"],"a":0,"why":"The lens changes shape to focus light.","no":["","The optic disc is where the optic nerve leaves the eye: the blind spot.","The utricle and saccule sense gravity and straight-line motion.","The tympanic membrane vibrates with sound at the end of the ear canal."],"id":"q145"},{"kc":"kcn31","q":"Which of these fills the space behind the lens and is never replaced?","c":["Vitreous humor","Utricle and saccule","Retina","Ciliary body"],"a":0,"why":"The vitreous humor fills the space behind the lens and is never replaced.","no":["","The utricle and saccule sense gravity and straight-line motion.","The retina holds the photoreceptors.","The ciliary body holds the zonules and makes aqueous humor."],"id":"q146"},{"kc":"kcn31","q":"Which of these is where the optic nerve leaves the eye: the blind spot?","c":["Optic disc","Ciliary body","Cornea","Nasolacrimal duct"],"a":0,"why":"The optic disc is where the optic nerve leaves the eye: the blind spot.","no":["","The ciliary body holds the zonules and makes aqueous humor.","The cornea is the clear front window of the fibrous tunic.","The nasolacrimal duct drains tears into the nasal cavity."],"id":"q147"},{"kc":"kcn32","q":"Which of these makes tears?","c":["Lacrimal gland","Vitreous humor","Lens","Basilar membrane"],"a":0,"why":"The lacrimal gland makes tears.","no":["","The vitreous humor fills the space behind the lens and is never replaced.","The lens changes shape to focus light.","The basilar membrane is the membrane the organ of Corti sits on."],"id":"q148"},{"kc":"kcn32","q":"Which of these drains tears into the nasal cavity?","c":["Nasolacrimal duct","Lens","Semicircular canals","Retina"],"a":0,"why":"The nasolacrimal duct drains tears into the nasal cavity.","no":["","The lens changes shape to focus light.","The semicircular canals sense rotation of the head.","The retina holds the photoreceptors."],"id":"q149"},{"kc":"kcn33","q":"Which of these vibrates with sound at the end of the ear canal?","c":["Tympanic membrane","Stapes","Basilar membrane","Semicircular canals"],"a":0,"why":"The tympanic membrane vibrates with sound at the end of the ear canal.","no":["","The stapes pushes on the oval window.","The basilar membrane is the membrane the organ of Corti sits on.","The semicircular canals sense rotation of the head."],"id":"q150"},{"kc":"kcn33","q":"Which of these pushes on the oval window?","c":["Stapes","Choroid","Basilar membrane","Nasolacrimal duct"],"a":0,"why":"The stapes pushes on the oval window.","no":["","The choroid is the dark vascular layer under the sclera.","The basilar membrane is the membrane the organ of Corti sits on.","The nasolacrimal duct drains tears into the nasal cavity."],"id":"q151"},{"kc":"kcn33","q":"Which of these bulges to release pressure from the cochlea?","c":["Round window","Stapes","Nasolacrimal duct","Choroid"],"a":0,"why":"The round window bulges to release pressure from the cochlea.","no":["","The stapes pushes on the oval window.","The nasolacrimal duct drains tears into the nasal cavity.","The choroid is the dark vascular layer under the sclera."],"id":"q152"},{"kc":"kcn34","q":"Which of these is the membrane the organ of Corti sits on?","c":["Basilar membrane","Semicircular canals","Retina","Lacrimal gland"],"a":0,"why":"The basilar membrane is the membrane the organ of Corti sits on.","no":["","The semicircular canals sense rotation of the head.","The retina holds the photoreceptors.","The lacrimal gland makes tears."],"id":"q153"},{"kc":"kcn34","q":"Which of these sense rotation of the head?","c":["Semicircular canals","Lacrimal gland","Optic disc","Lens"],"a":0,"why":"The semicircular canals sense rotation of the head.","no":["","The lacrimal gland makes tears.","The optic disc is where the optic nerve leaves the eye: the blind spot.","The lens changes shape to focus light."],"id":"q154"},{"kc":"kcn34","q":"Which of these sense gravity and straight-line motion?","c":["Utricle and saccule","Basilar membrane","Retina","Vitreous humor"],"a":0,"why":"The utricle and saccule sense gravity and straight-line motion.","no":["","The basilar membrane is the membrane the organ of Corti sits on.","The retina holds the photoreceptors.","The vitreous humor fills the space behind the lens and is never replaced."],"id":"q155"},{"kc":"kcn33","q":"Which of these is the spiral organ of hearing?","c":["Cochlea","Basilar membrane","Cornea","Vitreous humor"],"a":0,"why":"The cochlea is the spiral organ of hearing.","no":["","The basilar membrane is the membrane the organ of Corti sits on.","The cornea is the clear front window of the fibrous tunic.","The vitreous humor fills the space behind the lens and is never replaced."],"id":"q156"}],"cases":[{"kc":["kcn14"],"q":"A man wakes up with the left side of his face drooping. He cannot close the left eye or smile on that side, but he feels a cotton swab normally everywhere on his face. Which nerve is affected?","c":["Trigeminal (V)","Facial (VII)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Oculomotor (III)"],"a":1,"why":"Lost facial expression with intact facial sensation points to the facial nerve (Bell's palsy). The trigeminal carries sensation, which is normal.","no":["Trigeminal damage would remove facial sensation.","","XII moves the tongue.","III would affect eye movement and the eyelid, not the smile."],"title":"The drooping smile","id":"x1"},{"kc":["kcn12"],"q":"A 3-month-old's head circumference has jumped two sizes in a month, and the soft spot on top bulges. An ultrasound shows enlarged lateral and third ventricles but a normal fourth ventricle. Where is the block?","c":["Interventricular foramen","Cerebral aqueduct","Arachnoid granulations","Central canal of the cord"],"a":1,"why":"Lateral and third ventricles swollen, fourth normal: the block sits between the third and fourth ventricles, at the cerebral aqueduct. The infant's unfused skull lets the head grow.","no":["A block there would spare the third ventricle.","","A block at the granulations would enlarge all four ventricles.","The cord's canal is downstream of the fourth ventricle."],"title":"The growing head","id":"x2"},{"kc":["kcn19","kcn18"],"q":"After a diving accident, a patient can breathe on his own but cannot move his arms or legs. Which level of injury fits best?","c":["C1","Just below C5","L4","S2"],"a":1,"why":"Below C5, the phrenic roots (C3 to C5) still drive the diaphragm, but the brachial plexus (C5 to T1) and everything below are cut off.","no":["At C1 he would need a ventilator.","","An L4 injury would spare the arms.","S2 would spare the arms and most of the legs."],"title":"Breathing but not moving","id":"x3"},{"kc":["kcn21"],"q":"A patient has shooting pain from the buttock down the back of the thigh to the foot, worse when sitting. Which nerve is being irritated?","c":["Femoral","Sciatic","Obturator","Phrenic"],"a":1,"why":"Sciatica: the sciatic nerve (sacral plexus) runs from the buttock down the back of the thigh.","no":["The femoral nerve is in the front of the thigh.","","The obturator nerve is in the medial thigh.","The phrenic nerve goes to the diaphragm."],"title":"Pain down the back of the leg","id":"x4"},{"kc":["kcn29","kcn14"],"q":"After ear surgery, a patient finds food tastes flat on the front of the tongue on one side, though touch there feels normal. Which nerve carries the lost taste?","c":["Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Facial (VII)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Trigeminal (V)"],"a":1,"why":"Taste from the front two thirds of the tongue travels on the facial nerve; touch there is trigeminal, which is fine.","no":["IX carries taste from the back third.","","XII moves the tongue.","V carries touch from the tongue, which is normal."],"title":"Flat taste","id":"x5"},{"kc":["kcn34"],"q":"A drummer has played without ear protection for twenty years and now cannot hear high-pitched sounds. Why won't it come back?","c":["The ossicles fused","The cochlear hair cells were destroyed and do not regenerate","The eardrum is torn","The auditory tube is blocked"],"a":1,"why":"Loud noise kills cochlear hair cells, and human hair cells do not grow back.","no":["That is otosclerosis, a different, often fixable problem.","","A torn eardrum usually heals.","A blocked tube causes temporary muffling."],"title":"The drummer","id":"x6"},{"kc":["kcn34","kcn33"],"q":"After spinning in a chair and stopping suddenly, a child feels the room still turning. Which structures are responsible?","c":["Utricle and saccule","Semicircular canals","Cochlea","Auditory ossicles"],"a":1,"why":"The semicircular canals sense rotation; their fluid keeps moving after you stop, so the brain still senses turning.","no":["They sense tilt and straight-line motion.","","The cochlea senses sound.","The ossicles transmit sound."],"title":"The spinning room","id":"x7"},{"kc":["kcn10"],"q":"A stroke in the lower brainstem leaves a patient unable to breathe on his own, with unstable heart rate and blood pressure. Which structure was hit?","c":["Pons","Medulla oblongata","Cerebellum","Thalamus"],"a":1,"why":"The medulla houses the respiratory and cardiovascular centers.","no":["The pons helps relay and regulate, but the vital centers are in the medulla.","","The cerebellum coordinates movement.","The thalamus relays sensation."],"title":"The lower brainstem","id":"x8"},{"kc":["kcn31"],"q":"An older patient sees halos around lights, and the pressure inside the eye is high on testing. What is most likely blocked?","c":["The nasolacrimal duct","The canal of Schlemm","The optic chiasm","The ciliary zonules"],"a":1,"why":"Blocked aqueous drainage through the canal of Schlemm raises pressure: glaucoma.","no":["That drains tears outside the eye.","","The chiasm carries visual signals behind the eyes.","The zonules hold the lens."],"title":"Halos and high pressure","id":"x9"},{"kc":["kcn15"],"q":"A patient's left eye drifts outward and down, the left eyelid droops, and the left pupil is large. Which nerve is damaged?","c":["Abducens (VI)","Oculomotor (III)","Trochlear (IV)","Facial (VII)"],"a":1,"why":"III runs four of the six eye muscles, lifts the eyelid and constricts the pupil. Without it, the lateral rectus (VI) and superior oblique (IV) pull the eye out and down.","no":["VI only moves the eye outward.","","IV only runs the superior oblique.","VII closes the eye; it does not lift the lid or control the pupil."],"title":"Down and out","id":"x10"},{"kc":["kcn20"],"q":"A child falls on an outstretched arm and fractures the surgical neck of the humerus. Afterward, she cannot lift the arm out to the side and has a numb patch over the shoulder. Which nerve was injured?","c":["Radial","Axillary","Ulnar","Median"],"a":1,"why":"The axillary nerve wraps around the surgical neck and supplies the deltoid and the skin over it.","no":["The radial nerve runs further down, in the radial groove.","","The ulnar nerve runs at the elbow.","The median nerve runs down the front of the arm."],"title":"The shoulder fracture","id":"x11"},{"kc":["kcn2","kcn4"],"q":"A patient has lost all sensation over one strip of the chest on one side, but the muscles there work. Where is the damage most likely?","c":["One ventral root","One dorsal root (or its ganglion)","The motor cortex","The cerebellum"],"a":1,"why":"A single strip of skin is one dermatome, served by one dorsal (sensory) root. Motor function, via the ventral root, is spared.","no":["A ventral root lesion would weaken muscles.","","Cortical damage would not follow one dermatome.","The cerebellum does not carry sensation."],"title":"One stripe of numbness","id":"x12"}],"order":[{"kc":["kcn12"],"title":"The CSF path","prompt":"From where CSF is made to where it returns to the blood.","steps":["Choroid plexus","Lateral ventricles","Interventricular foramen","Third ventricle","Cerebral aqueduct","Fourth ventricle","Subarachnoid space","Arachnoid granulations","Dural venous sinuses"],"why":"Made by the choroid plexus, through the four ventricles, around the brain and cord, back into venous blood at the granulations.","id":"o1"},{"kc":["kcn23"],"title":"The reflex arc","prompt":"Put the five parts of a reflex arc in order.","steps":["Receptor","Sensory neuron","Integration center (cord gray matter)","Motor neuron","Effector"],"why":"In the knee jerk, the integration center is a single synapse, which is why it is so fast.","id":"o2"},{"kc":["kcn3"],"title":"From the vertebra in","prompt":"Move inward from the bone to the spinal cord.","steps":["Vertebra","Epidural space (fat, vessels)","Dura mater","Arachnoid mater","Subarachnoid space (CSF)","Pia mater","Spinal cord"],"why":"The epidural space is outside the dura; the CSF is between arachnoid and pia.","id":"o3"},{"kc":["kcn33","kcn34"],"title":"A sound reaching the brain","prompt":"Trace a sound from the air to the nerve.","steps":["Auricle","External acoustic meatus","Tympanic membrane","Malleus","Incus","Stapes","Oval window","Basilar membrane moves","Hair cells bend against the tectorial membrane","Cochlear nerve (VIII)"],"why":"Air vibrates the eardrum; the ossicles carry it to the oval window; fluid moves the basilar membrane and the hair cells fire the nerve.","id":"o4"},{"kc":["kcn30","kcn31"],"title":"Light reaching the brain","prompt":"Trace light from outside the eye to the brain.","steps":["Cornea","Aqueous humor","Pupil","Lens","Vitreous humor","Retina","Optic disc","Optic nerve (II)","Optic chiasm"],"why":"Cornea and lens focus the light on the retina; the signal leaves at the optic disc as the optic nerve.","id":"o5"},{"kc":["kcn29"],"title":"A smell reaching the brain","prompt":"Trace an odor to the cortex.","steps":["Odor molecule dissolves in nasal mucus","Olfactory receptor cell","Axons through the cribriform plate","Olfactory bulb","Olfactory tract","Olfactory cortex and limbic system (no thalamus)"],"why":"Smell is the one sense that skips the thalamus.","id":"o6"},{"kc":["kcn10"],"title":"The brainstem","prompt":"Top to bottom.","steps":["Midbrain","Pons","Medulla oblongata","Spinal cord"],"why":"The midbrain carries the colliculi, the pons bridges to the cerebellum, the medulla runs the vital centers.","id":"o7"},{"kc":["kcn5"],"title":"Wrapping a nerve","prompt":"From one axon to the whole nerve.","steps":["Axon","Endoneurium","Fascicle","Perineurium","Whole nerve","Epineurium"],"why":"Endo- inside, peri- around a fascicle, epi- on the outside of the nerve.","id":"o8"},{"kc":["kcn13"],"title":"The twelve cranial nerves","prompt":"I to XII.","steps":["Olfactory","Optic","Oculomotor","Trochlear","Trigeminal","Abducens","Facial","Vestibulocochlear","Glossopharyngeal","Vagus","Accessory","Hypoglossal"],"why":"Numbered front to back along the brain. Build the table early and drill it daily.","id":"o9"},{"kc":["kcn32"],"title":"Tears","prompt":"From where tears are made to where they drain.","steps":["Lacrimal gland (upper outer orbit)","Across the surface of the eye","Lacrimal sac (inner corner)","Nasolacrimal duct","Nasal cavity"],"why":"Made laterally, drained medially, which is why crying runs your nose.","id":"o10"}],"tables":[{"kc":["kcn13"],"title":"Sensory, motor or both","head":["Nerve","Number","Type"],"rows":[["Olfactory","I","Sensory"],["Optic","II","Sensory"],["Oculomotor","III","Motor"],["Trochlear","IV","Motor"],["Trigeminal","V","Both"],["Abducens","VI","Motor"],["Facial","VII","Both"],["Vestibulocochlear","VIII","Sensory"],["Glossopharyngeal","IX","Both"],["Vagus","X","Both"],["Accessory","XI","Motor"],["Hypoglossal","XII","Motor"]],"id":"t1"},{"kc":["kcn19"],"title":"The four plexuses","head":["Plexus","Roots","Named nerves"],"rows":[["Cervical","C1 to C4 (C5)","Phrenic"],["Brachial","C5 to T1","Musculocutaneous, axillary, radial, median, ulnar"],["Lumbar","L1 to L4","Femoral, obturator"],["Sacral","L4 to S4","Sciatic, tibial, common fibular"]],"id":"t2"},{"kc":["kcn20"],"title":"Brachial plexus targets","head":["Nerve","Supplies"],"rows":[["Musculocutaneous","Front of the arm (biceps)"],["Axillary","Deltoid"],["Radial","Back of the arm and forearm (extensors)"],["Median","Forearm flexors, thumb (carpal tunnel)"],["Ulnar","Little-finger side of the hand"]],"id":"t3"},{"kc":["kcn24","kcn25"],"title":"Sympathetic vs parasympathetic","head":["","Sympathetic","Parasympathetic"],"rows":[["Outflow","Thoracolumbar (T1 to L2)","Craniosacral (III, VII, IX, X; S2 to S4)"],["Ganglia","Near the cord (chain)","At or in the organ"],["Preganglionic fiber","Short","Long"],["Postganglionic fiber","Long","Short"],["Response","Fight or flight, body-wide","Rest and digest, local"]],"id":"t4"},{"kc":["kcn26"],"title":"Autonomic transmitters","head":["Fiber","Transmitter","Receptor"],"rows":[["All preganglionic","Acetylcholine","Nicotinic"],["Parasympathetic postganglionic","Acetylcholine","Muscarinic"],["Sympathetic postganglionic (most)","Norepinephrine","Adrenergic"],["Adrenal medulla (chromaffin cells)","Epinephrine","Into the blood"]],"id":"t5"},{"kc":["kcn27"],"title":"Somatic vs autonomic motor","head":["","Somatic","Autonomic"],"rows":[["Neurons to target","One","Two"],["Ganglion","None","Yes"],["Targets","Skeletal muscle","Smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, glands"],["Control","Voluntary","Involuntary"]],"id":"t6"},{"kc":["kcn30"],"title":"The three tunics","head":["Tunic","Structures"],"rows":[["Fibrous (outer)","Sclera and cornea"],["Vascular (middle)","Choroid, ciliary body, iris"],["Neural (inner)","Retina"]],"id":"t7"},{"kc":["kcn15","kcn32"],"title":"Extrinsic eye muscles","head":["Muscle","Nerve"],"rows":[["Lateral rectus","Abducens (VI)"],["Superior oblique","Trochlear (IV)"],["Medial rectus","Oculomotor (III)"],["Superior rectus","Oculomotor (III)"],["Inferior rectus","Oculomotor (III)"],["Inferior oblique","Oculomotor (III)"]],"id":"t8"},{"kc":["kcn33","kcn34"],"title":"Ear regions","head":["Region","Contents","Job"],"rows":[["Outer ear","Auricle, external acoustic meatus","Collect sound"],["Middle ear","Malleus, incus, stapes","Carry vibration to the oval window"],["Inner ear: cochlea","Organ of Corti","Hearing"],["Inner ear: vestibule","Utricle, saccule","Gravity, straight-line motion"],["Inner ear: canals","Three semicircular canals","Rotation"]],"id":"t9"},{"kc":["kcn9","kcn10","kcn11"],"title":"Brain structures and jobs","head":["Structure","Job"],"rows":[["Thalamus","Sensory relay to cortex"],["Hypothalamus","Homeostasis, pituitary"],["Pineal gland","Melatonin"],["Medulla oblongata","Heart rate, breathing"],["Cerebellum","Corrects movement"],["Corpus callosum","Joins the hemispheres"],["Hippocampus","New memories"],["Amygdala","Fear, emotional memory"]],"id":"t10"},{"kc":["kcn28"],"title":"Receptor types","head":["Receptor","Detects"],"rows":[["Chemoreceptor","Dissolved chemicals"],["Thermoreceptor","Temperature change"],["Photoreceptor","Light"],["Mechanoreceptor","Physical deformation"],["Baroreceptor","Vessel wall stretch"],["Nociceptor","Tissue damage"]],"id":"t11"}],"explain":[{"kc":["kcn1"],"prompt":"Explain why a needle can be placed safely between L3 and L4 to collect CSF.","model":"The adult spinal cord ends around L1 to L2 at the conus medullaris. Below that, the canal holds the cauda equina, nerve roots floating in CSF in the subarachnoid space. A needle placed lower enters that fluid, and the roots slide out of its way, so there is no cord to injure.","points":["Cord ends around L1 to L2","Below it: cauda equina (roots)","CSF in the subarachnoid space","Roots move aside; no cord to hit"],"id":"e1"},{"kc":["kcn8","kcn2"],"prompt":"Explain why gray matter is on the outside of the cerebrum but on the inside of the spinal cord.","model":"The cerebrum's job is computing, which needs enormous amounts of gray matter (cell bodies). Folding it on the outside as cortex packs the most surface into the skull, with white matter tracts beneath. The cord's main job is carrying long axon tracts between brain and body, so the myelinated white matter goes outside where there is room, and the gray matter for local reflexes sits protected in the core.","points":["Cerebrum: gray outside as folded cortex","Folding maximizes surface area","Cord: a cable of long tracts","White matter outside, gray core inside"],"id":"e2"},{"kc":["kcn25"],"prompt":"Explain why a sympathetic response feels like a whole-body event while parasympathetic effects stay local.","model":"Sympathetic ganglia sit close to the cord in the chain, so a short preganglionic fiber can spread up and down the chain to many long postganglionic fibers headed to many organs at once. Parasympathetic ganglia sit at or in the target organ, so one long preganglionic fiber reaches one organ, and the short postganglionic fiber acts only there.","points":["Sympathetic ganglia near the cord","Chain spreads one input to many organs","Parasympathetic ganglia at the organ","One pathway, one organ"],"id":"e3"},{"kc":["kcn29"],"prompt":"Trace an odor from the air to the brain, and explain what makes smell different from the other senses.","model":"Odor molecules dissolve in the nasal mucus and bind olfactory receptor cells. Their axons pass through the cribriform plate of the ethmoid to the olfactory bulb, then along the olfactory tract to the olfactory cortex and limbic system. Unlike every other sense, smell reaches the cortex without relaying through the thalamus.","points":["Receptor cells in the nasal epithelium","Axons through the cribriform plate","Olfactory bulb, then tract","Skips the thalamus"],"id":"e4"},{"kc":["kcn31"],"prompt":"Explain why everyone has a blind spot, and why you usually do not notice it.","model":"The optic disc is where the ganglion cell axons gather and leave the eye as the optic nerve, and where retinal vessels enter. There is no room for photoreceptors there, so light falling on it is not detected. You do not notice because the two eyes' blind spots fall on different parts of the scene and the brain fills in the gap.","points":["Optic disc: optic nerve exits, vessels enter","No photoreceptors there","The other eye covers the gap","The brain fills in"],"id":"e5"},{"kc":["kcn34"],"prompt":"Explain why hearing lost to years of loud noise does not come back.","model":"Sound moves the basilar membrane and bends the hair cells of the organ of Corti against the tectorial membrane. Loud noise damages and kills those hair cells. Human cochlear hair cells do not regenerate, so the loss is permanent.","points":["Hair cells of the organ of Corti","Loud noise destroys them","They do not regenerate","So the loss is permanent"],"id":"e6"},{"kc":["kcn12"],"prompt":"Explain why blocking CSF drainage makes a newborn's head enlarge rapidly, and why an adult's does not.","model":"CSF is made continuously by the choroid plexus. If its path is blocked, fluid backs up in the ventricles and pressure rises (hydrocephalus). In a newborn the skull bones are not yet fused, so the sutures and fontanels give and the whole head enlarges. An adult skull is fused, so the pressure squeezes the brain instead.","points":["CSF made continuously","Block backs fluid up: hydrocephalus","Newborn skull not fused","Head grows instead of brain being squeezed"],"id":"e7"},{"kc":["kcn14"],"prompt":"A patient cannot wrinkle the forehead on one side, but feels touch normally on that side of the face. Explain which nerve is affected and how you rule out the other candidate.","model":"Wrinkling the forehead is a muscle of facial expression, run by the facial nerve (VII). Facial sensation is carried by the trigeminal nerve (V). Since sensation is normal, the trigeminal is working, so the problem is the facial nerve.","points":["Expression muscles = facial nerve (VII)","Facial sensation = trigeminal (V)","Normal sensation rules out V"],"id":"e8"},{"kc":["kcn23"],"prompt":"Explain why the knee-jerk reflex is so fast, and why it still works in a patient whose brain cannot respond.","model":"The knee jerk is monosynaptic: the sensory neuron from the muscle spindle synapses directly on the motor neuron in the cord, with no interneuron, so there is only one synaptic delay (about 25 ms total). The whole circuit is in the spinal cord, so it does not need the brain.","points":["Monosynaptic: one synapse","No interneuron, so less delay","Circuit is entirely spinal","Brain not needed"],"id":"e9"}],"decks":[{"id":"dcn","icon":"🧠","prompt":"Say its name, whether it is sensory, motor or both, and what it does, then flip.","title":"Cranial nerve cards","desc":"Number, name, sensory or motor, and what each of the twelve does","cards":[{"id":"cn:I","front":"I","back":"Olfactory. Sensory only. Smell.","kc":"kcn13"},{"id":"cn:II","front":"II","back":"Optic. Sensory only. Vision.","kc":"kcn13"},{"id":"cn:III","front":"III","back":"Oculomotor. Motor only. Four of the six eye muscles, lifting the eyelid and constricting the pupil.","kc":"kcn13"},{"id":"cn:IV","front":"IV","back":"Trochlear. Motor only. The superior oblique muscle of the eye.","kc":"kcn13"},{"id":"cn:V","front":"V","back":"Trigeminal. Both sensory and motor. Facial sensation and chewing.","kc":"kcn13"},{"id":"cn:VI","front":"VI","back":"Abducens. Motor only. The lateral rectus, turning the eye outward.","kc":"kcn13"},{"id":"cn:VII","front":"VII","back":"Facial. Both sensory and motor. Facial expression, taste from the front two thirds of the tongue, tears and saliva.","kc":"kcn13"},{"id":"cn:VIII","front":"VIII","back":"Vestibulocochlear. Sensory only. Hearing and balance.","kc":"kcn13"},{"id":"cn:IX","front":"IX","back":"Glossopharyngeal. Both sensory and motor. Taste from the back third of the tongue and swallowing.","kc":"kcn13"},{"id":"cn:X","front":"X","back":"Vagus. Both sensory and motor. Parasympathetic supply to the heart, lungs and digestive tract, plus swallowing and voice.","kc":"kcn13"},{"id":"cn:XI","front":"XI","back":"Accessory. Motor only. The trapezius and sternocleidomastoid.","kc":"kcn13"},{"id":"cn:XII","front":"XII","back":"Hypoglossal. Motor only. Movement of the tongue.","kc":"kcn13"}]},{"id":"dnerve","icon":"⚡","prompt":"Say which plexus it comes from and what it supplies, then flip.","title":"Named nerve cards","desc":"The eleven named nerves: which plexus, and what each supplies","cards":[{"id":"nv:Phrenic","front":"Phrenic nerve","back":"Cervical plexus. Supplies the diaphragm.","kc":"kcn19"},{"id":"nv:Musculocutaneous","front":"Musculocutaneous nerve","back":"Brachial plexus. Supplies the front of the arm, including biceps brachii.","kc":"kcn20"},{"id":"nv:Axillary","front":"Axillary nerve","back":"Brachial plexus. Supplies the deltoid and teres minor.","kc":"kcn20"},{"id":"nv:Radial","front":"Radial nerve","back":"Brachial plexus. Supplies the back of the arm and forearm, the extensors.","kc":"kcn20"},{"id":"nv:Median","front":"Median nerve","back":"Brachial plexus. Supplies most forearm flexors and the thumb muscles, through the carpal tunnel.","kc":"kcn20"},{"id":"nv:Ulnar","front":"Ulnar nerve","back":"Brachial plexus. Supplies the little-finger side of the hand and most small hand muscles.","kc":"kcn20"},{"id":"nv:Femoral","front":"Femoral nerve","back":"Lumbar plexus. Supplies the front of the thigh, the quadriceps.","kc":"kcn21"},{"id":"nv:Obturator","front":"Obturator nerve","back":"Lumbar plexus. Supplies the medial thigh, the adductors.","kc":"kcn21"},{"id":"nv:Sciatic","front":"Sciatic nerve","back":"Sacral plexus. Supplies the back of the thigh, then the leg through its two branches.","kc":"kcn21"},{"id":"nv:Tibial","front":"Tibial nerve","back":"Sacral plexus. Supplies the back of the leg and the plantar flexors.","kc":"kcn21"},{"id":"nv:Common fibular","front":"Common fibular nerve","back":"Sacral plexus. Supplies the front and side of the leg, the dorsiflexors.","kc":"kcn21"}]}]};
